<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/agency> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "agency"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.1" ;
  skos:definition "A person's capacity and power to act, make decisions, and shape their environment according to their values, interests, and goals."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Exercising agency requires genuine opportunities that enable individuals to pursue what they consider valuable. Consequently, families, professionals, and researchers must promote the agency of girls and women with disabilities by ensuring they have greater opportunities to make decisions about their own lives and transform social reality."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-1> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/autonomy>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/empowerment>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/meaningful-participation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/autonomy> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "autonomy"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.2" ;
  skos:definition "The capacity of persons to shape and pursue their own lives, values, and commitments, and to participate meaningfully in decisions that affect them."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It is not merely freedom from external interference or dependence on others, but a socially and relationally constituted capacity, whose exercise requires adequate material conditions, recognition, supportive relationships, access to knowledge, and protection from domination, manipulation, and oppression."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-1> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/relational-autonomy>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/supported-autonomy> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/agency>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/legal-capacity> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/relational-autonomy> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "relational autonomy"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.2.1" ;
  skos:definition "The capacity to act and orient one’s life that emerges from relations of interdependence between individuals, material contexts, and social structures, rather than from a presumed individual self-sufficiency."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "In this perspective, autonomy is not an intrinsic property of the subject, but a situated condition that is continuously negotiated through the relations between people, environments, and institutions."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/autonomy> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/interdependence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/supported-autonomy>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/care> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/supported-autonomy> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "supported autonomy"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.2.2" ;
  skos:definition "A person’s ability to make and act on their own choices with the information, assistance, and accessible conditions they need, while their wishes and preferences remain central."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Scholars emphasise that dependence on others for assistance does not negate autonomy but rather, when based on respect and consent, enhances agency."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/autonomy> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/agency>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/relational-autonomy>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support-in-decision-making> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/care> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "care"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.3" ;
  skos:definition "The ethical and legal recognition of the relational responsibilities that support human dignity and autonomy, conceived not as dependence or paternalism, but as a rights‑based practice ensuring inclusion, assistance, and mutual respect within society."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Feminist theories have been crucial in reshaping the concept of care, by highlighting it as a central social and ethical practice rather than private, gendered labour. They exposed how traditional views undervalued caregiving, redefined care as a source of moral knowledge and social cohesion, and promoted the idea that interdependence and relational responsibility are fundamental to justice and equality. The ethics of care highlight that vulnerability and interdependence are universal human conditions and that care should be organised socially and politically, not left to private charity or individual sacrifice. In this perspective, the CRPD and EU law can be read as institutional embodiments of the ethics of care. The CRPD reframes care as a means of empowerment and inclusion, emphasising personal assistance, community living, and the shared responsibility of states, families, and societies."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-1> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/caregiver> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/unpaid-work>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/interdependence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/supported-autonomy>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/relational-autonomy> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/caregiver> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "caregiver"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.3.1" ;
  skos:definition "Someone who provides ongoing care or support to a person who needs assistance because of age, illness, or disability, whether paid or unpaid."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "In both legal and philosophical debate, caregiving in the context of disability has shifted from a dependency narrative to a relational and empowerment-based view. The EU legal framework recognises carers’ rights through equality, employment, and social protection law,particularly via the Work–Life Balance Directive and the Court of Justice’s Coleman case"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/care> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/supported-autonomy>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/unpaid-work> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/deinstitutionalisation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "deinstitutionalisation"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.4" ;
  skos:definition "The transition from institutional care to community-based services and independent living, with appropriate support, autonomy, and social inclusion."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It is not limited to moving people out of facilities: it entails transforming the system to prevent new admissions, restoring legal capacity, guaranteeing housing, personal assistance and access to mainstream services, and providing redress to those who suffered institutionalisation. The CRPD derives it from art. 19, and in 2022 the Committee adopted Guidelines on deinstitutionalisation, including in emergencies. It specifically affects women and children with disabilities."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-1> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/institutionalisation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/independent-living-and-inclusion-in-the-community>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/alternative-care>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/situations-of-risk>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/children-with-disabilities> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/empowerment> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "empowerment"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.5" ;
  skos:definition "Expansion of freedom of choice and action that involves increasing one’s authority and control over the resources and decisions that affect one’s life."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It can also be defined as the act of enabling people to act on their own in order to reach their self-defined goals. Empowerment could be in different facets, ranging from economic, political, social to psychological. The empowerment of historically marginalised groups, such as women, yields positive outcomes not only for the groups themselves but also for the society as a whole of which they are a part, contributing to more inclusive, equitable, and sustainable social development."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-1> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/psychological-empowerment>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-empowerment> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/agency>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/autonomy>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/meaningful-participation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/psychological-empowerment> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "psychological empowerment"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.5.1" ;
  skos:definition "Individual’s cognitive state characterised by a sense of perceived control, competence, and goal internalisation."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It is considered as a multi-faceted construct reflecting the different dimensions of being psychologically enabled, and is conceived of as a positive integrate of perceptions of personal control, a proactive approach to life, and a critical understanding of the socio-political environment, which is rooted firmly in a social action framework that includes community change, capacity building, and collectivity."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/empowerment> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/agency>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/recovery> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-empowerment> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "social empowerment"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.5.2" ;
  skos:definition "The process through which individuals and groups develop the skills, capabilities, and autonomy necessary to make decisions that challenge and reduce social inequalities."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It involves fostering the autonomy of marginalised populations, allowing them to actively participate in shaping their own lives and communities."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/empowerment> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/meaningful-participation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-inclusion> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/independence> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "independence"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.6" ;
  skos:definition "The capacity of persons to exercise meaningful control over their lives, make decisions, and pursue their own purposes without being subject to undue domination, coercion, or arbitrary interference."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The normative liberal ideal of a self-sufficient entails a subject who is presumed capable of acting, deciding, and sustaining their life without reliance on others or on supportive social and material conditions. In this view, agency is treated as an individual property and autonomy as a state of detachment from relations of dependence, often obscuring the social, infrastructural, and relational conditions that make action possible. However, in a different view, independence does not require self-sufficiency or the absence of dependence on others. Rather, independence is exercised within relations of interdependence and relies on social, material, institutional, and infrastructural conditions that enable persons to act and participate on terms compatible with dignity and equality"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-1> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/relational-autonomy>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/interdependence> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/independent-living-and-inclusion-in-the-community> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "independent living and inclusion in the community"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.7" ;
  skos:definition "The right of persons with disabilities to live independently and to be included in the community, choosing where, how and with whom to live on an equal basis, without being obliged to live in a particular arrangement."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It does not mean living alone or coping without help: independence is exercised with the necessary supports, such as personal assistance or accessible community services. The CRPD recognises it in art. 19, developed by General Comment No. 5 (2017), which requires deinstitutionalisation and the provision of community-based services. It is the opposite of institutionalisation, which segregates and depersonalises."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-1> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/deinstitutionalisation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/alternative-care>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/situations-of-risk>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/legal-capacity>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/personal-assistance>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/person-centred-support> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/infantilisation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "infantilisation"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.8" ;
  skos:definition "Treatment that reduces an adult person to the status of a minor, denying her authority, privacy, sexuality and decision-making capacity."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "In the context of disability, it can include using a childish tone, making decisions for someone without consulting them, or assuming they cannot understand or express their own preferences. It characteristically affects persons with disabilities – and, in an aggravated way, women with disabilities –who are portrayed as eternal minors, asexual or incapable of forming a judgement, which may serve to justify paternalism, the substitution of their will and the restriction of their sexual, reproductive and participation rights. It is not benevolent care, but a form of discrimination based on stereotypes that heightens exposure to violence and abuse. International law requires combating it: the CRPD requires action against stereotypes and prejudices (art. 8), recognises legal capacity on an equal basis (art. 12) and places the person's autonomy and dignity at its core (art. 3)."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-1> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/overprotection> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-stereotypes>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/legal-capacity>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/guardianship>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-rights> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/overprotection> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "overprotection"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.8.1" ;
  skos:definition "The restriction of activities and decisions presented as protection, but which limits autonomy, participation and personal development."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/infantilisation> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/autonomy>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/agency> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/institutionalisation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "institutionalisation"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.9" ;
  skos:definition "The placement of people in residential institutions, where they often live separately from the wider community and have limited autonomy and control over their daily lives."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-1> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/deinstitutionalisation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/independent-living-and-inclusion-in-the-community>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/segregation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/interdependence> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "interdependence"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.10" ;
  skos:definition "The condition in which subjects are mutually constituted and sustained through networks of social, material, and institutional relations."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Rather than undermining agency, interdependence is the condition that enables it: individuals act and orient their lives through relationships with others, environments, infrastructures, and norms that both constrain and support their capacity to act."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-1> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/agency>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/relational-autonomy>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/independence> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/meaningful-participation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "meaningful participation"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.11" ;
  skos:definition "The genuine involvement of women and girls in the design, implementation and evaluation of actions, with the ability to influence decisions."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-1> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/agency>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/empowerment>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/participatory-research> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/personal-assistance> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "personal assistance"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.12" ;
  skos:definition "A form of support for people with disabilities that focuses on self-determination, in which the person receiving assistance decides how the assistance is organised and utilised. The goal is to enable people to lead as self-determined a life as possible and to participate in society on an equal footing."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It involves supporting the person with a disability in performing daily activities, moving around, and participating in social and professional life, enabling the person with a disability to live independently. It provides support in everyday activities that the person cannot perform independently, such as dressing, personal care, leaving the house, getting around town, administering medication, shopping, maintaining relationships with friends, and performing professional work. A personal assistant is not a caregiver. They are a person who supports the person with a disability in their daily functioning, enabling them to lead an independent and active life. A personal assistant acts in accordance with the will of the person they support, respecting their independence and choices. The scope of their support is tailored to the individual needs of the person using the personal assistance service"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-1> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/independent-living-and-inclusion-in-the-community>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/will-and-preferences> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "support"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.13" ;
  skos:definition "The broad range of measures, relationships, resources, services, accommodations, and assistance that enable individuals to exercise their rights, make decisions, pursue their goals, participate in society, and live according to their own preferences and will."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Support may be provided formally or informally and can vary in intensity, duration, and form according to individual circumstances and contexts It is based on the recognition that all human beings may require different forms of assistance throughout their lives and that the need for support should not diminish a person's autonomy, dignity, legal status, or right to self-determination."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-1> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/formal-support>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/informal-support>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/natural-support>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/person-centred-support>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support-in-decision-making>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support-network> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/supported-autonomy>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/personal-assistance> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/formal-support> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "formal support"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.13.1" ;
  skos:definition "Support provided through organised systems, services, programmes, institutions, professionals, or public and private bodies."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Formal support is typically regulated, funded, or structured within legal, social, educational, health, employment, or community frameworks."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/informal-support>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/personal-assistance> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/informal-support> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "informal support"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.13.2" ;
  skos:definition "Support provided through personal relationships and social networks outside formal service structures."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Informal support is generally based on family relationships, friendships, peer networks, community connections, and mutual aid."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/formal-support>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/natural-support>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support-network> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/natural-support> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "natural support"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.13.3" ;
  skos:definition "Relationships, resources, and forms of assistance that arise organically within everyday environments and communities rather than being organised through specialised services or programmes."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Natural supports strengthen social inclusion, belonging, reciprocity, and participation in community life."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/informal-support>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support-network>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-inclusion> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/person-centred-support> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "person-centred support"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.13.4" ;
  skos:definition "An approach to support that is directed by the individual's own goals, preferences, rights, aspirations, and life choices."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Person-centred support recognises the individual as the primary decision-maker regarding the nature, extent, and manner in which support is provided and emphasises autonomy, participation, dignity, and inclusion."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/personal-assistance> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support-in-decision-making> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "support in decision-making"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.13.5" ;
  skos:definition "The set of measures, relationships and forms of assistance that enables individuals to understand information, consider options, communicate preferences, and make decisions according to their own will and preferences. Decision-making support respects personal autonomy and recognises that all individuals have the right to direct their own lives."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It may include support from trusted persons or support networks, assistance with communication, accessible information, interpretation, facilitation or advance planning. The CRPD (art. 12), as developed by General Comment No. 1 (2014), requires replacing substitute decision-making with a support model guided by the person's will and preferences, proportionate to their circumstances and accompanied by safeguards against abuse, conflict of interest and undue influence."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/legal-capacity>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/will-and-preferences>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/safeguards>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/guardianship> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support-network> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "support network"@en ;
  skos:notation "1.13.6" ;
  skos:definition "The collection of individuals, relationships, organisations, services, and resources that provide practical, emotional, social, informational, or decision-making assistance to a person."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Support networks may include both formal and informal sources of support and may evolve over time according to the person's needs, circumstances, and preferences."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/formal-support>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/informal-support>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/interdependence> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/alternative-care> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "alternative care"@en ;
  skos:notation "2.1" ;
  skos:definition "Forms of care for a child deprived of their family environment, or who cannot remain in it in their own interest: foster care, kafalah, residential care and, as a more permanent measure creating a parent-child bond, adoption."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "They are chosen in accordance with the child's best interests and the principles of necessity and suitability, with a preference for family- and community-based solutions. The CRC governs alternative care in art. 20 and adoption in art. 21, complemented by the UN Guidelines for the Alternative Care of Children (2009). For children with disabilities, the CRPD requires preventing their abandonment and segregation, not separating them on the ground of disability and, where they cannot remain with their immediate family, securing their care within the extended family or, failing that, in a family setting within the community (arts. 23.3 to 23.5, in conjunction with art. 19)."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-2> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/best-interests-of-the-child>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/parental-responsibility>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/deinstitutionalisation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/children-with-disabilities> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/best-interests-of-the-child> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "best interests of the child"@en ;
  skos:notation "2.2" ;
  skos:definition "A requirement that, in any decision affecting a child or adolescent, their best interests be assessed and weighed as a primary consideration."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It does not operate as a rule of automatic precedence, but as a criterion to be determined case by case, in the light of each child's circumstances and oriented towards the effective enjoyment of their rights; hence its threefold nature as a substantive right, an interpretative principle and a rule of procedure. Where children with disabilities are concerned, this determination must be made on a basis of equality and non-discrimination, with respect for the evolving capacities of the child and for their identity, and ensuring that they can express their views with appropriate support and the accessibility needed for effective participation."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-2> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/right-of-the-child-to-be-heard> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/children-with-disabilities> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "children with disabilities"@en ;
  skos:notation "2.3" ;
  skos:definition "Children and adolescents with disabilities recognised as holders of all rights on an equal basis with other children, and not merely as objects of protection or care."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The CRPD sets this out in art. 7 around three axes: the full enjoyment of their rights without discrimination, the best interests of the child as a primary consideration, and their right to express their views freely on all matters affecting them, receiving disability- and age-appropriate assistance to do so. To this is added respect for their evolving capacities and their identity (art. 3(h)). They are particularly exposed to abandonment, institutionalisation, violence and abuse, and educational exclusion; their protection requires inclusive education, support for families, accessibility and deinstitutionalisation, avoiding their separation from family on the ground of disability. For girls with disabilities, a gender dimension is also present."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-2> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/best-interests-of-the-child>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/right-of-the-child-to-be-heard>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/alternative-care> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/family> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "family"@en ;
  skos:notation "2.4" ;
  skos:definition "A social group formed through bonds of care, support, kinship, and responsibility."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Family is not limited to heterosexual, nuclear, or biologically related forms, but includes diverse structures such as single-parent families, extended families, chosen families, queer families, adoptive families, co-parenting networks, and other communities of care."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-2> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/care>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/parental-responsibility>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/right-to-marry-and-to-found-a-family> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/parental-responsibility> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "parental responsibility"@en ;
  skos:notation "2.5" ;
  skos:definition "The set of duties and powers of those who care for, raise, educate, represent and protect a child, always exercised in the child's interest and with respect for the child's evolving capacities."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It is a function oriented towards the child's well-being, not a power over the child; in some legal systems it takes the form of national institutions such as parental authority. Where the parents have a disability, it also includes the right to receive appropriate assistance in carrying out their child-rearing responsibilities, as well as the prohibition on separating a child from their parents on the ground of disability."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-2> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/child-custody> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/best-interests-of-the-child>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/right-of-the-child-to-be-heard> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/child-custody> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "child custody"@en ;
  skos:notation "2.5.1" ;
  skos:definition "The habitual living arrangement with the child and their day-to-day care."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Both its allocation – sole or joint – and the regime of contact with the other parent are decided in accordance with the child's best interests. The existence of violence must weigh in that decision, and the disability of a parent or of the child cannot, in itself, be a ground for denying custody or justifying separation; parents with disabilities must be provided with appropriate assistance to exercise it."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/parental-responsibility> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/best-interests-of-the-child>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/right-of-the-child-to-be-heard> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/right-of-the-child-to-be-heard> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "right of the child to be heard"@en ;
  skos:notation "2.6" ;
  skos:definition "The right of every child or adolescent to express their views freely on matters affecting them and to have those views given due weight in accordance with their age and maturity, in any setting and not only before the courts."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It includes the right to be heard in judicial and administrative proceedings, whether directly or through a representative or an appropriate body. For children with disabilities, it encompasses the use of any mode, means or format of communication – sign language, braille, augmentative and alternative communication – and the availability of accessible environments."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-2> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/best-interests-of-the-child>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/meaningful-participation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accessibility> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/right-to-marry-and-to-found-a-family> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "right to marry and to found a family"@en ;
  skos:notation "2.7" ;
  skos:definition "The right of every person of marriageable age to marry and form a family with free and full consent, and to decide on having children."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Disability cannot restrict this right: persons with disabilities have the right to marry and to found a family, to decide freely on the number and spacing of their children, to retain their fertility and not to be separated from their children on the ground of disability. Marriage equality is not a uniform European standard but an option of national law; that said, States must provide some form of legal recognition and protection for same-sex couples."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-2> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/parental-responsibility>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reproductive-rights>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/forced-sterilisation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/cultural-relativism> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "cultural relativism"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.1" ;
  skos:definition "A methodological and cognitive view according to which all human practices, beliefs, values, and behaviours can be properly understood and evaluated only within the context of the culture they occur in."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "This position categorically rejects the evaluation of cultural phenomena through the prism of one's own group's standards (so-called ethnocentrism) and assumes the equality of all cultural systems in shaping their internal rules."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/culture>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/ethnocentrism> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/culture> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "culture"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.2" ;
  skos:definition "A dynamic and public system of meanings embodied in symbols, constituting the overarching interpretive framework through which people give meaning to their experience and orient their actions in the world."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/cultural-identity> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/cultural-relativism>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/ethnocentrism> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/cultural-identity> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "cultural identity"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.2.1" ;
  skos:definition "A multidimensional and intersectional concept that refers to a sense of belonging to a particular group based on various cultural categories, including nationality, language, ethnicity, race, gender, sexual orientation, disability, and religion."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It is constructed and maintained through the sharing of collective knowledge, such as traditions, heritage, language, aesthetics, norms, and customs. Moreover, it is a dynamic, multidimensional and relational concept, as it evolves over time through individuals’ exposure to and interaction with diverse cultural groups."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/culture> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/cultural-integration>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectionality> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/decolonial-studies> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "decolonial studies"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.3" ;
  skos:definition "A field of research that emerged in the 1990s and developed a critique of colonialism by highlighting how colonial power structures persist and continue to reproduce, over time, the marginalising effects imposed upon oppressed subjects."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Colonial dynamics are thus identified as one of the paradigms of marginalisation observable within broader – and still contemporary – relations of power."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/postcolonial-studies> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/ethnocentrism> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "ethnocentrism"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.4" ;
  skos:definition "The tendency to perceive one’s own culture as the only valid one, and to evaluate the beliefs, values and behaviours of individuals or groups from other cultures according to the criteria of one’s own cultural framework."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "This perspective often leads to an overestimation of the merits of one’s own culture and a consequent underestimation or devaluation of other cultures."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/cultural-relativism>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/racism-and-racialisation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/stereotypes> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/homelessness> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "homelessness"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.5" ;
  skos:definition "A relatively permanent, involuntary life situation in which an individual or family is left homeless or unable to provide, through their efforts, the minimal shelter they could consider their own home."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "In the literature, homelessness signifies the simultaneous severing of social ties, loss of roots, and the destruction of personal identity, caused by a combination of structural (economic) and individual (biographical) factors."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/homeless-woman> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/poverty>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/precarity> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/homeless-woman> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "homeless woman"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.5.1" ;
  skos:definition "A woman experiencing a specific, multidimensional existential and relational crisis, involving the severing of family ties and the trauma of domestic violence, which constitutes the main etiological factor of female homelessness."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "A characteristic defining element is the concept of hidden homelessness – women are less likely to exist on the street and more likely to live with loved ones or maintain dysfunctional relationships in exchange for shelter. Institutionally, this concept strongly correlates with homeless mother syndrome, determined by isolation in support facilities (e.g., single mothers' homes), a crisis in caregiving roles, and institutional anxiety. This phenomenon is operationally described by the European ETHOS classification, which categorises women's homelessness through the prism of lack of shelter, lack of housing, and existence in precarious or inadequate housing conditions."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/homelessness> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/domestic-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/feminisation-of-poverty> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusion--law> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "inclusion (law)"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.6" ;
  skos:definition "Inclusion is the legal obligation to provide equal access, eliminate discrimination, and ensure it involves actively removing barriers, promoting equity, and fostering a sense of belonging across all areas of life, including the workplace, education, and family settings and refers to a comprehensive social and organizational approach."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "In the UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities, inclusion is both a general principle (art. 3) and a specific right applicable to various areas (e.g. daily life, art. 19; education, art. 24; employment, art. 27; society and culture, arts. 29–30)."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-inclusion> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/equality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accessibility> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-inclusion> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "social inclusion"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.6.1" ;
  skos:definition "The process of ensuring that all persons, especially those who face disadvantage, discrimination, or marginalisation, can participate fully and meaningfully in social, economic, political, cultural, and civic life."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It requires not only formal access to institutions and opportunities, but also the removal of material, legal, institutional, and attitudinal barriers that limit participation, recognition, belonging, and the effective exercise of rights."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusion--law> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-integration>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/meaningful-participation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/integration--migration> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "integration (migration)"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.7" ;
  skos:definition "The process through which individuals and groups gain access to, participate in, and contribute to a society’s institutions, social relationships, and cultural life."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/cultural-integration>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-integration>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-integration> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/migration> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/cultural-integration> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "cultural integration"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.7.1" ;
  skos:definition "Cultural integration involves the transformation of norms, values, and behaviours through interactions among individuals from diverse cultural backgrounds."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Unlike structural integration, which is unidirectional — where greater participation in societal institutions reflects higher integration — cultural integration is bidirectional, spanning from cultural assimilation to multiculturalism."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/integration--migration> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/assimilation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/differentialism>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/interculturalism>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/multiculturalism> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/assimilation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "assimilation"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.7.1.1" ;
  skos:definition "A one-sided process in which immigrants replace their original culture with that of the host country, aiming for cultural homogeneity."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/cultural-integration> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/interculturalism>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/multiculturalism> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/differentialism> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "differentialism"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.7.1.2" ;
  skos:definition "An approach according to which immigrants either self-segregate or are excluded from the host culture, forming distinct ethnic subsocieties with their own norms and values."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/cultural-integration> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/segregation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/multiculturalism> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/interculturalism> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "interculturalism"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.7.1.3" ;
  skos:definition "An approach to integration that emphasises dialogue, interaction, and cooperation among people from different cultural backgrounds."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/cultural-integration> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/assimilation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/multiculturalism> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/multiculturalism> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "multiculturalism"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.7.1.4" ;
  skos:definition "An approach to integration that recognises and accommodates cultural diversity while promoting equal participation in society."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/cultural-integration> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/differentialism>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/interculturalism> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-integration> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "social integration"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.7.2" ;
  skos:definition "Participation in personal relationships and social networks, such as friendships, marriages, and membership in voluntary organisations (interactional integration), and the individual’s sense of belonging to the host society (identificational integration)."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/integration--migration> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-inclusion>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-group> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-integration> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "structural integration"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.7.3" ;
  skos:definition "Migrants’ participation in and access to key institutions within the host society, such as the labour market, housing, and welfare system."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Immigrants are considered structurally integrated when they attain positions comparable to those of native citizens."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/integration--migration> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusion--employment>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/welfare-benefit> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/meritocracy> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "meritocracy"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.8" ;
  skos:definition "A system of social organisation in which social positions are allocated on the basis of individual merit, disregarding factors such as social background, gender, ethnicity, or other forms of social differentiation."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Under this system, individuals are considered responsible for their own outcomes and life choices. Consequently, social status is perceived as a reflection of personal worth and achievement, being interpreted as either success or failure."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/equality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/affirmative-actions> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/migration> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "migration"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.9" ;
  skos:definition "The movement of people from one place of residence to another, usually for an extended period."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It may take place within a country or across national borders. People migrate for many reasons, including the search for better living conditions, escape from armed conflict, education and employment. Migration may be temporary, including seasonal movement, or permanent. Common forms include emigration (leaving a country), immigration (entering a country), re-emigration (returning after a temporary period abroad), repatriation (the return of citizens or their descendants from abroad with support from a state or other institution), resettlement (a state-organised transfer of people within its borders) and deportation (a legally authorised removal from a country)."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/refugee>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/integration--migration>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/racism-and-racialisation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/mobility-justice> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "mobility justice"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.10" ;
  skos:definition "The principle that people should be able to move, or remain where they are, safely and freely, without their opportunities being unfairly limited by social, economic, or political barriers."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Mobility constitutes a fundamental factor in ensuring access to spaces and services in everyday life. The possibility of moving is conditioned not only by factors related to the individual (such as age and health), but also by cultural, social, economic, political, and environmental factors. Consequently, in order to reduce inequalities generated by an inequitable distribution of mobility opportunities, it is necessary to intervene in the broader set of factors that produce them."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accessibility>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/spatial-justice>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/architectural-and-sensory-barrier> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/postcolonial-studies> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "postcolonial studies"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.11" ;
  skos:definition "A field of research that investigates how colonialism has shaped power relations within exploited territories, influencing the representation, language, and culture of oppressed subjects, as well as structurally shaping the overall range of their possibilities and aspirations."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Postcolonial studies differ from decolonial studies. While postcolonial studies examine the continuing effects of colonialism on culture, identity, and representation, decolonial studies focus on challenging the colonial structures of power and knowledge that remain embedded in modern society. 158 - 159]"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/decolonial-studies> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/poverty> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "poverty"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.12" ;
  skos:definition "A condition in which people lack the resources and real opportunities needed to meet basic needs, enjoy an adequate standard of living, and participate in society."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "An intersectional reading treats poverty not as an individual attribute but as an effect of interlocking structures of gender, disability, racialisation, migration status and class. These axes do not simply add up; they produce qualitatively distinct positions that single-axis statistics render invisible. EU data show elevated risk for women (21.9% vs. 19.8% for men), persons with disabilities (28.8%) and non-EU citizens (43.8%). Consequences extend beyond material scarcity to restricted capabilities (Sen, 1999), health inequalities, educational exclusion and intergenerational transmission. Anti-poverty policy must therefore be difference-sensitive rather than compensating isolated characteristics."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/feminisation-of-poverty> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/precarity>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/welfare-benefit> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/feminisation-of-poverty> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "feminisation of poverty"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.12.1" ;
  skos:definition "A process in which women and girls are at greater risk of poverty, its deepening, or its persistence due to gender inequalities in access to income, employment, resources, property, and social protection, as well as the unequal burden of unpaid care work."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It refers not only to the higher proportion of women among people experiencing poverty, but also to the gendered nature of the mechanisms that produce and sustain poverty. In the case of women and girls with disabilities, this risk may be intensified by barriers to education and employment, discrimination, limited access to services and benefits, and additional disability-related costs. The feminisation of poverty can be analysed through income and its sources, employment, economic dependency, care responsibilities, access to benefits and services, housing conditions, and the material consequences of disability."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/poverty> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-inequality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/unpaid-work> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/precarity> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "precarity"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.13" ;
  skos:definition "A socially and politically produced condition in which some populations face greater exposure to harm or violence because they have less access to social and economic support."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Precariousness and precarity are constitutive conditions of human experience. On one hand, describing a life as precarious entails recognising the condition of precariousness as a fundamental aspect of one’s experience. On the other hand, precarity denotes the political circumstances wherein specific populations endure the breakdown of social and economic networks of support resulting in disparate vulnerability to harm or violence. While precariousness is an ontological feature of humanness, precarity is the outcome of political processes of “differential distribution of exposure and dependency”."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/poverty>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/vulnerability> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/properness> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "properness"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.14" ;
  skos:definition "Condition of those subjects whose presence in a public space is deemed acceptable because they conform to the expectations of those who observe or regulate that space, or to a normative framework."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Judgments of properness are grounded in moral, aesthetic, cultural, class-based, and economic status criteria, among others, which are at the base of the regulation."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/paradigmatic-subject>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/surveillance>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/territorial-stigmatisation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/refugee> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "refugee"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.15" ;
  skos:definition "The forced life situation and legal status of a person forced to leave their country of origin due to a well-founded fear of persecution, armed conflict, violence, or other events seriously threatening their life, health, and freedom."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "According to the Convention Relating to the Status of Refugees, 1951, a refugee is a person outside their country who is unable or unwilling to return there due to a well-founded fear of persecution for five specific reasons: race, religion, nationality, membership in a particular social group, or political opinion. Legally and sociologically, refugee status is characterised by three main elements: forced migration, crossing a state border (individuals fleeing to safer regions of their country are called internally displaced persons (IDPs), and loss of protection from their home government. Refugee status is associated with special international protection, including the fundamental principle of non-refoulement, which prohibits the return of such individuals to places where they would face danger."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/migration>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/situations-of-risk>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/human-rights-based-approach> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-group> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "social group"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.16" ;
  skos:definition "A collection of two or more individuals who interact with one another, share a sense of identity or belonging, and are connected through shared purposes, goals, norms, values, or expectations."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Individuals may belong to multiple social groups simultaneously, and these groups may vary in duration and structure. Three types of social groups can be defined: 1) primary groups, which are small, intimate, emotionally close groups such as families or close friends; 2) secondary groups, which are larger, more impersonal, goal‑oriented groups such as work teams or organisations, and 3) reference groups, which are groups that individuals use as standards for self‑evaluation. Social groups differ from aggregates, which consist of people gathered in the same place without meaningful interaction, and from categories, which include people who share a characteristic but lack interaction or a shared identity."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/cultural-identity>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-integration>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/minoritised-groups> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-isolation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "social isolation"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.17" ;
  skos:definition "The restriction of contacts, relationships, community participation or access to support networks"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support-network>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-inclusion>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/mental-health> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/spatial-justice> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "spatial justice"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.18" ;
  skos:definition "The fair distribution of resources, services, and opportunities across places, together with people’s ability to access and use them regardless of where they live"@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Spatial justice studies focus on how social inequalities are produced, manifested, and reproduced through space and its configuration, which is itself shaped by urban policies and planning practices. It also investigates how space can become a terrain for claims, redistribution, and the guarantee of rights."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/mobility-justice>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accessibility>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/segregation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/surveillance> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "surveillance"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.19" ;
  skos:definition "A set of strategies and infrastructures deployed for the control of space and of individuals."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/urban-security>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/properness>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/territorial-stigmatisation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/territorial-stigmatisation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "territorial stigmatisation"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.20" ;
  skos:definition "The process through which negative narratives associated with a place are transferred onto the people who live there, producing exclusion and marginalisation."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It is produced through the stratification of ethnic and class discrimination, control policies, media processes, and urban and social policies."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/stigma>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/segregation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/spatial-justice> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/urban-safety> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "urban safety"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.21" ;
  skos:definition "A condition in which perceived risk relates to situations dependent on factors that are not directly controllable, such as natural events or diseases."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/urban-security>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/situations-of-risk>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accessibility> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/urban-security> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "urban security"@en ;
  skos:notation "3.22" ;
  skos:definition "It refers both to the perception of a condition in which risks deriving from intentional acts against persons and property are absent, and to the set of measures adopted within an urban environment to reduce the likelihood of such acts occurring."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/urban-safety>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/surveillance>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/spatial-justice> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/ableism> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "ableism"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.1" ;
  skos:definition "Discrimination, prejudice, or bias against people with disabilities, based on the assumption that typical physical, neurocognitive, or sensory abilities are superior or more desirable."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The term derives from able + -ism and began to emerge around the mid-1960s within social movements advocating for disability rights. It was later theorised and more systematically developed in the 1980s within the field of disability studies to describe both individual attitudes and structural forms of oppression embedded in social institutions, cultural norms, media narratives, and everyday practices."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-4> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/stereotypes>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/stigma> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accessibility> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "accessibility"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.2" ;
  skos:definition "A condition ensuring that persons with disabilities have access, on an equal basis with others, to: the physical environment; transportation; information and communications, including information and communications technologies and systems; other facilities and services open or provided to the public, both in urban and in rural areas. This includes the identification and elimination of obstacles and barriers, covering buildings, roads, schools, housing, medical facilities, workplaces, electronic services, and emergency services."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Although its predominant use places particular emphasis on the architectural aspect, the concept of accessibility also encompasses organisational and cultural dimensions. In relation to the organizational dimension, accessibility requires removing barriers embedded in rules, procedures, schedules, communication practices, and resource allocation. This means, for example, providing flexible ways to participate and ensuring that information is clear. In relation to the cultural dimension, accessibility requires challenging prejudices, language, and established practices that may exclude some people, while promoting listening, recognition, and a sense of belonging. Accessibility therefore also involves the effective opportunity to be recognised as a legitimate voice and to influence the life of the organisation."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-4> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/digital-accessibility> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reasonable-accommodation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/universal-design> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/digital-accessibility> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "digital accessibility"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.2.1" ;
  skos:definition "Digital accessibility is a feature of websites, mobile applications, and the documents published within them (e.g., PDF files) that enables independent and convenient use by all users, including those with visual, hearing, mobility, or cognitive disabilities."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accessibility> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/universal-design>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/usability> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/autism> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "autism"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.3" ;
  skos:definition "A form of neurodivergence, understood as a neurocognitive variation from dominant cultural norms of thinking, communicating, processing sensory information and experiencing the world."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Autism is not an illness, and it is not equally disabling for every person. However, inaccessible environments, social expectations and a lack of appropriate support can create barriers that disable autistic people. When autism remains unrecognised or a person’s needs are unsupported over a long period, this can negatively affect their wellbeing and contribute to anxiety, depression, autistic burnout and suicidal thoughts. Recognition, acceptance, accessible environments and individualised support can improve wellbeing and participation."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-4> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/neurodivergence> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/barrier> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "barrier"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.4" ;
  skos:definition "Factors within physical, material, social, or symbolic environments that hinder participation on an equal basis with others."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The CRPD, in its preamble, recognises that disability results from the interaction between persons with impairments and social barriers that hinder their full and effective participation in society on an equal basis with others."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-4> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/architectural-and-sensory-barrier>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/barrier-in-education--education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/personal-barriers>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-barrier> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/biopsychosocial-model-of-disability>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/levers-facilitators-of-inclusion> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/architectural-and-sensory-barrier> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "architectural and sensory barrier"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.4.1" ;
  skos:definition "A physical obstacle or sensory factor that causes discomfort, imposes limitations, or prevents individuals from using spaces and buildings when they have, for any reason, reduced or impaired physical abilities, whether permanent or temporary."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The same effect can also result from the absence of measures or signage that help individuals orient themselves, identify locations, and recognise potential hazards"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/barrier> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accessibility>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/universal-design>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/mobility-justice> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/barrier-in-education--education> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "barrier in education (education)"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.4.2" ;
  skos:definition "Conditions within the educational environment that hinder or limit everyone’s ability to access, participate, learn and develop on an equal footing, regardless of their characteristics, needs or circumstances."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Generally speaking, these include attitudinal barriers linked to stereotypes, discrimination or low expectations; institutional barriers arising from educational policies that lack inclusivity, a lack of resources, or poor coordination and organisation of support services; curricular barriers, linked to inflexible teaching methods or rigid assessment practices; and barriers relating to accessibility and participation."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/barrier>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-5> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusive-education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/universal-design-for-learning>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reasonable-accommodation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/personal-barriers> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "personal barriers"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.4.3" ;
  skos:definition "Beliefs, attributions, and perceptions that individuals hold about themselves and that may limit their ability to make decisions, participate actively, and exercise autonomy."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/barrier> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/self-stigma>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/psychological-empowerment>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/autonomy> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-barrier> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "social barrier"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.4.4" ;
  skos:definition "stigma, prejudice, stereotyping, low expectations, and discriminatory practices that marginalise disabled individuals in education, employment, healthcare, and social interactions."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Unlike physical or environmental obstacles, social barriers are rooted in how society perceives and treats disability, often viewing it as a personal deficit rather than a result of exclusionary structures. Social barriers manifest in various ways: inaccessible communication, lack of representation in decision-making, patronising attitudes, and institutional policies that fail to accommodate diverse needs. Overcoming them requires cultural change, awareness-raising, and the active involvement of disabled people in shaping inclusive practices and policies."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/barrier> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/ableism>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/stereotypes>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-discrimination> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/disability> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "disability"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.5" ;
  skos:definition "A complex and evolving concept that cannot be fully captured by a single, context-independent definition; its meaning varies according to the models through which it is understood and the social, cultural and institutional contexts in which it is used."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-4> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/models-of-disability>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/visible-and-non-visible-disability> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/impairment>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/barrier>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/ableism> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/models-of-disability> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "models of disability"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.5.1" ;
  skos:definition "Conceptual frameworks used to understand and interpret disability, explaining its causes, meanings, and the ways society responds to it."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/disability> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/medical-model-of-disability>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-model-of-disability>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/biopsychosocial-model-of-disability>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/neurodiversity-paradigm> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/medical-model-of-disability> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "medical model of disability"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.5.1.1" ;
  skos:definition "A model of disability that conceives disability as a defective or pathological condition of the individual, inevitably entailing disadvantages both at the personal level and at the relational and social levels, and which must therefore be “treated” and, where possible, “repaired” through the instruments of medicine and technology, taking as its standard reference a presumed normal state of health and human functioning."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Although it has never been the subject of a genuine theoretical elaboration, this paradigm has most profoundly permeated the narratives and practices surrounding disability (including legal ones) and continues to find implicit legitimisation within them, despite giving rise to deep-seated ableist prejudices and manifest forms of discrimination."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/models-of-disability> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/ableism> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-model-of-disability> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "social model of disability"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.5.1.2" ;
  skos:definition "A model of disability that conceives disability as the result of socially produced barriers and oppressive structures that restrict the rights, equality and full participation in society of persons with disabilities."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Theorised by Mike Oliver in the 1990s and, somewhat later, by Thomas Shakespeare, who proposed a significant variant of it, it can today be regarded as an “umbrella” paradigm encompassing approaches that focus on the oppressive mechanisms through which disability is socially constructed. These approaches aim at the removal of environmental and cultural barriers that hinder the enjoyment of the rights to freedom and equality and, more generally, the full participation of persons with disabilities in society."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/models-of-disability> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-barrier>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accessibility>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/medical-model-of-disability> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/biopsychosocial-model-of-disability> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "biopsychosocial model of disability"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.5.1.3" ;
  skos:definition "An intermediate model of disability, regarded as the normative expression of the UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (See CRPD), which conceives disability as produced through the interaction between people with long-term physical, mental, intellectual or sensory impairments and attitudinal and environmental barriers that hinder their full and effective participation in society on an equal basis with others."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/models-of-disability> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/impairment>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/barrier>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-model-of-disability> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/neurodiversity-paradigm> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "neurodiversity paradigm"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.5.1.4" ;
  skos:definition "An approach broadly aligned with the social model of disability that conceives neurodiversity a “natural and valuable form of human diversity”, focusing on the nervous system as an embodied phenomenon."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It promotes the redefinition of social arrangements through neuroqueering in order to guarantee neurocognitive and embodied freedom and to meet the broad accessibility needs of neurodivergent persons."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/models-of-disability> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/autism>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/neurodivergence> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/visible-and-non-visible-disability> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "visible and non-visible disability"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.5.2" ;
  skos:definition "Disabilities whose effects are, respectively, readily apparent to others or not immediately observable. Visible disabilities may involve recognizable physical, sensory, or communicative characteristics, although their nature and impact cannot be inferred from appearance alone. Non-visible disabilities include physical, sensory, cognitive, neurological, psychological, or chronic conditions that may significantly affect a person’s daily activities and social participation without being externally evident."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The distinction concerns the social visibility of disability, not its existence, severity, or legitimacy. Visibility may also vary across contexts and over time: the same disability may be apparent in some circumstances and non-visible in others. In both cases, disability is shaped by the interaction between individual conditions and environmental, institutional, and attitudinal barriers."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/disability> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/long-term-illness>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/stigma> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/impairment> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "impairment"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.6" ;
  skos:definition "A loss or limitation of physical, mental, sensory, or cognitive function that affects a person’s body or mind, forming one component of disability when interacting with social and environmental barriers."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Impairment concerns an organism (its functioning and structure) that, as a result of damage, does not function properly. Impaired is defined as any abnormality or loss of ability in the structure or functioning of an organism in psychological, psychophysical, or anatomical terms. It is a condition understood in biomedical terms that relates directly to the structure of an organism that, as a result of a defect or injury, does not function properly. It is usually certified by medical experts and medical services."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-4> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/disability>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/medical-model-of-disability>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/biopsychosocial-model-of-disability> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/levers-facilitators-of-inclusion> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "levers/facilitators of inclusion"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.7" ;
  skos:definition "Factors that promote, support, and facilitate the full participation in society of all individuals in an equal basis."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-4> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/barrier> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/long-term-illness> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "long-term illness"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.8" ;
  skos:definition "A health condition of extended duration which may result in lasting physical or psychological impairments that affect an individual’s ability to participate fully in professional and social life."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "According to the case law of the Court of Justice of the European Union, a long-term illness may qualify as a disability where it entails long-lasting physical, mental, or psychological impairments which, in interaction with various barriers, hinder full participation in work. The Court of Justice of the European Union furthermore has increasingly clarified that the effects of long‑term health conditions must be assessed in light of the prohibition of direct and indirect discrimination and the requirement to adopt appropriate measures to ensure equal treatment"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-4> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/impairment>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reasonable-accommodation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/direct-discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/indirect-discrimination> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/neurodivergence> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "neurodivergence"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.9" ;
  skos:definition "A term coined in the 1990s within autistic community to refer to divergences from dominant cultural norms of neurocognitive functioning."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-4> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/neurodiversity-paradigm> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/non-paradigmatic-subject> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "non-paradigmatic subject"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.10" ;
  skos:definition "Anyone who does not reflect the characteristics commonly associated with the majority of the population or with specific socially defined categories of individuals."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-4> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/paradigmatic-subject>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/ableism>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/exclusion--robotics> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/paradigmatic-subject> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "paradigmatic subject"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.11" ;
  skos:definition "The individual to whom rationality and the abilities presumed to characterise the majority of the population are attributed, or those associated with specific categories of people defined on the basis of distinctive characteristics."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-4>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-9>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-12> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/non-paradigmatic-subject>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/universal-design>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/situated-knowledge> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/universal-design> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "universal design"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.12" ;
  skos:definition "The design of products, environments, programmes and services to be usable by all people, to the greatest extent possible, without the need for adaptation or specialised design, which does not exclude assistive devices where they are needed."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It is a proactive approach that incorporates human diversity from the very outset of a project. It requires a holistic and interdisciplinary approach to address diversity bias and ensures that technology is \"balanced, inclusive, and efficient\" for both majority and marginal groups. It shifts the focus from designing for a \"paradigmatic subject\" (the presumed majority) to intentionally including non-paradigmatic subjects, such as those with diverse body shapes, health conditions, or marginalised identities. The CRPD defines it in art. 2 and requires its promotion (art. 4.1 (f) and (g)); it is closely related to accessibility (art. 9 and General Comment No. 2), since it makes it possible to conceive accessible environments from the start, and it differs from reasonable accommodation, which is individual and reactive: the better the universal design, the fewer subsequent accommodations are needed. The term is associated with Ronald Mace and the seven principles formulated by the Center for Universal Design; its usefulness extends beyond disability and also benefits older persons and those facing temporary limitations. As a design approach, it aims at balanced, inclusive and efficient environments and products for non-paradigmatic subjects."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-4> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/universal-design-for-learning> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accessibility>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reasonable-accommodation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/assistive-technology--robotics> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/universal-design-for-learning> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "universal design for learning"@en ;
  skos:notation "4.12.1" ;
  skos:definition "A model that aims to reshape education by providing a conceptual framework that facilitates the analysis and evaluation of curriculum designs and educational practices, in order to identify barriers to learning and promote inclusive teaching approaches."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It sets out a framework for developing flexible curricular proposals (objectives, assessment, methodologies and resources) that respond to the diverse needs and abilities of pupils. Recognising the variability among individuals requires teaching approaches that are designed with the full range of differences in mind, using flexible curricular and organisational approaches. Universal Design for Learning is based on three principles: (1) providing multiple ways of engagement, (2) providing multiple forms of representation, and (3) providing multiple forms of action and expression."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/universal-design>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-5> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusive-education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/barrier-in-education--education> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/coeducation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "coeducation"@en ;
  skos:notation "5.1" ;
  skos:definition "An educational model that promotes a non-sexist approach to teaching and learning by recognising and valuing the contributions of women and men on an equal basis."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Its primary aim is to foster the full development and empowerment of both girls and boys, free from gender stereotypes and discriminatory practices. Coeducation goes beyond the concept of mixed-sex schooling, as research has shown that gender inequalities continue to persist within such educational settings. It entails the integration of the values, experiences, knowledge, and contributions of women and men throughout the educational process, thereby challenging and dismantling traditional gender hierarchies. It seeks to prepare students for active participation in both the public and private spheres of life, assigning equal social and educational value to each. Furthermore, coeducational practices create learning environments in which historically marginalised or invisible perspectives, experiences, and forms of knowledge are acknowledged and incorporated into the curriculum. This approach adopts a holistic perspective, addressing not only the needs and development of students but also the broader educational community, including teachers, families, and institutional structures"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-5> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-inequality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-stereotypes>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/education> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/difference-sensitive-pedagogy> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "difference-sensitive pedagogy"@en ;
  skos:notation "5.2" ;
  skos:definition "A pedagogical fundamental orientation and a professional stance promoting a reflective treatment of differences between learners that neither levels difference out (equal treatment as homogenisation) nor uses it for hierarchisation or stigmatisation."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Difference is recognised as the normal case; the aim is equal worth in difference. The term functions as an umbrella concept spanning several sub-concepts that share this underlying norm but operate on distinct levels of reference, each carrying its own guiding question, understanding of difference, and relation to power."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-5> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/diversity--education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/heterogeneity--education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusion--education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectionality--education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/personalisation--education> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectionality> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/diversity--education> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "diversity (education)"@en ;
  skos:notation "5.2.1" ;
  skos:definition "The variety of social affiliations (e.g. gender, ethnicity, social origin, religion, disability) and their appreciative recognition."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "As a sub-concept of difference-sensitive pedagogy, diversity is affirmatively charged: variety is framed as a resource and a value. This differentiates it from the more descriptive notion of heterogeneity The term originates in the Anglo-American organisational and anti-discrimination context and refers to the level of an organisation / institution. Diversity targets organisational cultures and institutional relations of recognition, rather than the single child or the overarching societal structure. However, intaking power relations into account the concept is weak or ambivalent. There is a risk that the affirmative framing can convert relations of inequality and power into a harmonising \"rhetoric of diversity\". Another point of criticism in critical scholarship is that diversity is seen as amenable to neoliberal \"diversity-management\" logics that render variety usable without addressing structural discrimination."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/difference-sensitive-pedagogy> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/heterogeneity--education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectionality--education> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/heterogeneity--education> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "heterogeneity (education)"@en ;
  skos:notation "5.2.2" ;
  skos:definition "The dissimilarity of the members of a learning group with respect to relevant attributes (e.g. attainment, language, social background, gender)."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "As a sub-concept of difference-sensitive pedagogy the term is initially descriptive — it describes variation without itself evaluating it — but is frequently given a normative turn in school pedagogy (\"heterogeneity as opportunity\"). Heterogeneity is an observational category for the composition of groups and refers to the learning group / class (meso level of difference) . The principal hazard is reification: by marking certain attributes as \"heterogeneity-relevant,\" social distinctions may be produced and entrenched rather than merely depicted. The very idea of a \"normal\" learner against which variation is measured is historically constructed. Concerning the dimension of power the concept seems neutral-descriptive on the surface, yet the term is relative to the assumed homogeneity and thus tends to convey a perspective centred on norms and deficits."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/difference-sensitive-pedagogy> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/diversity--education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/personalisation--education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/paradigmatic-subject> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusion--education> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "inclusion (education)"@en ;
  skos:notation "5.2.3" ;
  skos:definition "The full participation of all persons in education, irrespective of individual prerequisites."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It shifts the logic from adapting the individual to the system (integration) towards adapting the system to the diversity of all learners. Inclusion is at once a descriptive concept and a normative, human-rights claim: The concept addresses as well normative human-rights as at the same the system- and the institution. Thus inclusion formulates a goal and a yardstick for the design of educational systems. The concept is normative and action-oriented. Inclusion construes the difference that leads to exclusion as a barrier to participation to be overcome, thereby making relations of power and exclusion an object of pedagogical and policy change. Critical scholarship cautions that \"inclusion\" can be co-opted unless it confronts the structures that produce exclusion."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/difference-sensitive-pedagogy> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusive-education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusion--law>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/barrier-in-education--education> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectionality--education> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "intersectionality (education)"@en ;
  skos:notation "5.2.4" ;
  skos:definition "Intersectionality denotes the interlocking of social categories of difference and inequality (e.g. gender, class, ethnicity, disability), which do not stand side by side additively but mutually reinforce, attenuate, or modify one another."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Unlike other concepts of difference-sensitive pedagogics – like personalisation, heterogeneity, inclusion and (in attenuated form) diversity – intersectionality places relations of power and inequality explicitly at its centre. It thereby supplies the analytical vocabulary for the vertical interlocking of difference that remains under-determined in the other concepts. As an analytical and critical concept, it does not ask what categories of difference exist, but rather how they interact and mutually constitute one another (interdependence), thereby giving rise to social inequality, discrimination and privilege. Crucially: not additive (“woman + migrant + poor”) but interwoven. Intersectionality thus introduces the dimension of power and structure into the discussion that other concepts like heterogeneity and diversity lack."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/difference-sensitive-pedagogy> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectionality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectional-discrimination> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/personalisation--education> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "personalisation (education)"@en ;
  skos:notation "5.2.5" ;
  skos:definition "The orientation of learning provision towards the individual person — that is, attending to each learner's prerequisites, interests, pace, and meaning-making."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "As a sub-concept of difference-sensitive pedagogy, personalisation is analytically distinct from individualisation (organisational internal differentiation) and differentiation (methodological variation), even though the terms frequently blur in practice. Personalisation addresses difference at the level of the single person, not of the group or of societal categories. Difference is regarded as individual singularity: each person develops a distinct perspective through their circumstances (cf. the assumption of uniqueness alongside equal rights). Difference is here construed as personal rather than social-categorical. The critical blind spot of the concept is, that a purely personalised perspective can render structural inequality invisible by translating social categories into individual attributes."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/difference-sensitive-pedagogy> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/heterogeneity--education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/universal-design-for-learning>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusive-education> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/education> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "education"@en ;
  skos:notation "5.3" ;
  skos:definition "An intentional process that supports learning and the development of knowledge, understanding, skills, competences, attitudes and values through communication and organised activities."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "For statistical purposes, UNESCO’s International Standard Classification of Education (ISCED 2011) provides an operational framework for classifying education programmes and qualifications across countries. It covers formal and non-formal education throughout life. Informal learning is excluded from measures of participation in education, although recognised qualifications obtained through it may count towards educational attainment. Beyond its statistical meaning, education is recognised as a fundamental human right. UNESCO’s Rethinking Education: Towards a Global Common Good? (2015) proposes understanding education as a common good, emphasising its individual and collective purposes. These perspectives address complementary questions: how education is classified and measured, and what purposes it should serve."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-5> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/formal-education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusive-education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/informal-education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/non-formal-education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/special-education> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/lifelong-learning> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/formal-education> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "formal education"@en ;
  skos:notation "5.3.1" ;
  skos:definition "A structured and state-regulated educational process delivered through the different levels of the education system."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It is characterised by a hierarchical organisation, with regulated learning environments, fixed schedules, prescribed curricula, and formal assessment procedures. Its primary purpose is to provide accredited education leading to officially recognised qualifications, which are valued by both the education system and the labour market. Formal education is primarily delivered in schools and other recognised educational institutions."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/education> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/informal-education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/non-formal-education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/lifelong-learning> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusive-education> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "inclusive education"@en ;
  skos:notation "5.3.2" ;
  skos:definition "An educational principle that recognises, values, and responds to learner diversity."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Its goal is to eliminate social exclusion resulting from discriminatory attitudes and practices related to race, social class, ethnicity, religion, gender, and disability. Grounded in the belief that education is a fundamental human right and a foundation for a more just society, inclusive education is conceived as a continuous process of identifying and removing barriers to students' presence, participation, and achievement. It places particular emphasis on learners who are at heightened risk of marginalisation, exclusion, or underperformance."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/education> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusion--education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/universal-design-for-learning>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/special-education> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/informal-education> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "informal education"@en ;
  skos:notation "5.3.3" ;
  skos:definition "The lifelong process through which individuals acquire knowledge, skills, values, attitudes, and competencies through everyday interactions with their physical and social environments."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Unlike formal or non-formal education, it is neither planned nor intentionally designed for educational purposes. Instead, learning occurs naturally through processes of socialisation, adaptation to the environment, and participation in daily life. Informal education takes place in a variety of contexts, including the family, the community, peer relationships, the media, and other everyday social and environmental experiences."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/education> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/formal-education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/non-formal-education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/lifewide-learning> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/non-formal-education> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "non-formal education"@en ;
  skos:notation "5.3.4" ;
  skos:definition "A wide range of organised educational activities that take place outside the formal education system."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "These activities are intentionally planned and systematically structured to complement formal education and address the evolving lifelong learning needs of contemporary societies. Non-formal education is delivered in diverse settings and across flexible timeframes, including community organisations and educational institutions. It encompasses activities such as extracurricular programmes, workshops, seminars, and continuing professional development courses. Key components include literacy and post-literacy classes, vocational skills training, and continuing education designed to promote community and national development where formal education systems sometimes fail to meet societal needs, leading to the exploration of alternative educational models It provides vital second-chance learning to out-of-school youth and adults."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/education> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/formal-education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/informal-education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/lifelong-learning> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/special-education> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "special education"@en ;
  skos:notation "5.3.5" ;
  skos:definition "A form of schooling designed for students with disabilities, that may be delivered in special education schools or through specialised classrooms and programmes located within mainstream schools."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities establishes the right to inclusive education for all learners and, therefore, the right of all children to receive an education in the same classrooms. The incorporation of the principle of inclusive education into educational legislation requires a reconsideration of both the role and functions of special education schools and the educational responses provided by mainstream schools."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/education> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusive-education> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/lifelong-learning> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "lifelong learning"@en ;
  skos:notation "5.4" ;
  skos:definition "All learning activity undertaken throughout life, which results in improving knowledge, know-how, skills, competences and/or qualifications for personal, social and/or professional reasons"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-5> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/lifewide-learning> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/formal-education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/non-formal-education> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/lifewide-learning> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "lifewide learning"@en ;
  skos:notation "5.4.1" ;
  skos:definition "learning, either formal, non-formal or informal, that takes place across the full range of life activities (personal, social or professional) and at any stage of life”."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "“Lifewide learning is a dimension of lifelong learning"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/lifelong-learning> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/informal-education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/non-formal-education> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/affirmative-actions> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "affirmative actions"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.1" ;
  skos:definition "Specific, targeted, and temporary measures adopted by public authorities or private actors, aimed at preventing, reducing, or compensating disadvantages affecting certain protected groups identified on prohibited grounds of discrimination (racial or ethnic origin; religion or belief; disability; age; sexual orientation; sex, gender in order to ensure substantive and effective equality, rather than merely formal equality."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It constitutes a lawful derogation from the principle of equal treatment, insofar as it allows differential treatment justified by the need to correct factual inequalities, provided that it complies with the principles of proportionality, reasonableness, and temporariness. In the UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities, the right to benefit from affirmative action is set out in Article 5."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-6> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/equality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/bias> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "bias"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.2" ;
  skos:definition "A systematic deviation from rational judgment or objective assessment that arises from the use of cognitive shortcuts (heuristics), leading individuals to make predictable errors in perception, estimation, reasoning, or decision-making under conditions of uncertainty"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-6> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/algorithmic-bias--automated-systems> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/stereotypes>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/algorithmic-bias--automated-systems> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "algorithmic bias (automated systems)"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.2.1" ;
  skos:definition "The unfair systematic preference or prejudice encoded into automated systems, often reflecting societal stereotypes or flawed data collection."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Anchored in EU anti-discrimination law, it specifically refers to the unfair positive or negative treatment of individuals based on protected grounds such as sex, race, ethnic origin, religion, disability, age, or sexual orientation"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/bias>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-12> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination-by-proxy> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "discrimination"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.3" ;
  skos:definition "A conduct or policy that, in any area of public or private life, treats differently individuals who are situated in the same way without objective and reasonable justification or, conversely, treats similarly individuals who are situated differently, with the purpose or effect of nullifying or curtail the equal recognition, enjoyment, or exercise of one or more human rights and fundamental freedoms. Discrimination may be based on nationality; age; sex; sexual orientation; gender identity and expression; language; religion; cultural identity; political opinions or opinions of any kind; social origin; socioeconomic status; educational level; migrant, refugee, repatriate, stateless or internally displaced status; disability; genetic trait; mental or physical health condition, including infectious-contagious condition and debilitating psychological condition; or any other condition."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "In equality and human rights law, the concept of discrimination has evolved from a narrow focus on personal attributes to a more nuanced understanding that recognises how prejudice and disadvantage operate through relationships, perceptions, and social structures. The legal function of anti-discrimination rules is twofold: (1) to protect individuals from being subject to arbitrary or prejudicial distinctions and (2) to promote a society in which structural disparities and historic patterns of marginalisation are actively dismantled. In this sense, non-discrimination operates not only as a negative duty (to refrain from discrimination) but also as a positive obligation (to take measures ensuring effective equality). According to the CRPD, also the denial of reasonable accommodation constitutes discrimination. This principle extends equality into the realm of substantive justice, recognising that identical treatment can perpetuate inequality if it ignores individual differences."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-6> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/direct-discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination-by-association>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination-by-assumption-or-perception>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination-by-proxy>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/indirect-discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectional-discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/multiple-discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-discrimination> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/equality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reasonable-accommodation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/direct-discrimination> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "direct discrimination"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.3.1" ;
  skos:definition "Discrimination that occurs when one person is treated less favourably than another in a comparable situation because of a prohibited ground. For example, refusing to hire someone because of their disability or denying service due to race, all the other conditions being the same, constitutes direct discrimination."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The discriminatory intent or motive is not necessary; what matters is the causal connection between the ground and the less favourable treatment. However, some constitutional systems, such as the United States, traditionally required proof of discriminatory intent under the Equal Protection Clause, though this has been widely criticised for limiting redress against institutional discrimination."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/indirect-discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/employment-discrimination> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination-by-association> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "discrimination by association"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.3.2" ;
  skos:definition "Discrimination that occurs when an individual is treated less favourably, harassed, or otherwise disadvantaged because of their association with a person possessing a protected characteristic, such as race, religion, disability, sex, age, or sexual orientation."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The key feature is that the discriminatory conduct is motivated by bias against the protected group but directed at someone linked or related to that group. This concept first achieved explicit legal recognition in European Union law through the landmark judgment of the Court of Justice of the European Union (CJEU) in Coleman v. Attridge Law and Another (Case C‑303/06, 2008). The Court held that discrimination under Directive 2000/78/EC (the Employment Equality Framework Directive) is not limited to those who personally possess a protected characteristic. It also applies to situations where a person suffers adverse treatment because of their relationship with someone who does, such as a parent caring for a disabled child. The legal reasoning behind discrimination by association is grounded in the recognition that discriminatory motives operate relationally."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/caregiver>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination-by-assumption-or-perception> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination-by-assumption-or-perception> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "discrimination by assumption (or perception)"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.3.3" ;
  skos:definition "Discrimination that occurs when an individual is treated unfavourably because they are perceived, presumed, or stereotyped as having a protected characteristic, even if that perception is incorrect."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "What matters is the discriminator’s belief or assumption, not the person’s actual status. This type of discrimination acknowledges that prejudice often arises from perception and stereotype, rather than factual attributes. Equality law therefore protects individuals against being targeted due to mistaken beliefs about their identity. Whether the attribute assumed is “real” or “misattributed” is irrelevant; the harm lies in being subjected to biased treatment because of what others assume about one’s identity. The CRPD, by extending protection to persons with impairments or perceived impairments, also implicitly embraces this approach (Article 1 and Article 5)."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/stereotypes>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/bias> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination-by-proxy> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "discrimination by proxy"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.3.4" ;
  skos:definition "Discrimination that occurs through a proxy trait rather than direct reference to the characteristic itself (such as postal code, income level, accent, or surname), where those factors function as statistical surrogates for race, ethnicity, or disability (e.g. health insurance exclusions based on genetic markers correlated with particular ethnic minorities)."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The rationale is that equality law prohibits not just overt bias but also functional equivalents that reproduce the same disadvantage. In the era of algorithmic decision-making, proxy discrimination has gained renewed importance. Automated systems trained on biased data often reproduce discriminatory correlations."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/algorithmic-bias--automated-systems> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/indirect-discrimination> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "indirect discrimination"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.3.5" ;
  skos:definition "Discrimination that arises when a seemingly neutral provision, criterion, or practice disproportionately disadvantages persons with a protected characteristic, unless the measure is objectively justified by a legitimate aim and the means of achieving it are appropriate and necessary. For example: a workplace rule requiring full-time on-site presence may disproportionately exclude employees with disabilities or caregiving responsibilities if reasonable adjustments could achieve the same goal without exclusion."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "This concept, developed in U.S. equal protection and EU equality law, recognises that discrimination often operates structurally, through rules and decisions that perpetuate inequality even without explicit bias"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/direct-discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reasonable-accommodation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectional-discrimination> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "intersectional discrimination"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.3.6" ;
  skos:definition "A form of discrimination in which multiple protected characteristics — such as sex, gender, race, ethnicity, disability, age, religion, sexual orientation — interact in a manner that produces qualitatively distinct and compounded disadvantages."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "These disadvantages cannot be fully understood, proven, or remedied by isolating each ground of discrimination and examining it independently. In particular, legal systems structured around single-axis analysis (e.g., discrimination based solely on gender or solely on race) risk excluding or under-protecting those whose experiences do not fit neatly within one category."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectionality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/multiple-discrimination> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/multiple-discrimination> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "multiple discrimination"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.3.7" ;
  skos:definition "Any situation in which a person is subjected to discrimination on the basis of two or more protected characteristics, where such discrimination results in a distinct or aggravated disadvantage compared to discrimination based on a single ground."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Unlike intersectional discrimination, in which the intersection of the axes of discrimination renders the effects of each of them indistinguishable and inseparable, in multiple discrimination, discrimination on the basis of each axis can be identified independently of the others."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectional-discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectionality> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-discrimination> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "structural discrimination"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.3.8" ;
  skos:definition "A set of systemic, institutional, cultural, and spatial mechanisms and relations through which inequalities and relations of domination are produced, normalised, and reproduced over time, relatively independently of individual intentions."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "These dynamics operate organically at a structural and hegemonic level, configuring relations and practices that consolidate power hierarchies among social groups and embed them within the economic, political, and cultural structures of a given social formation. The geo-historically situated forms that result generate conditions of subordination and disadvantage for specific social groups, rendering them ordinary and difficult to recognise and contest."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-intersectionality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/segregation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/equality> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "equality"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.4" ;
  skos:definition "The principle and practice of ensuring that all people have the same dignity, rights, value, and opportunities, regardless of gender, sex, sexuality, race, class, disability, ethnicity, religion, age, or other aspects of identity."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Equality does not simply mean treating everyone in the same way (formal equality) but recognising historical and structural injustices and working to remove the barriers that prevent full participation, safety, and self-determination for marginalised people (substantive equality)."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-6> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/fairness>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/affirmative-actions>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reasonable-accommodation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/fairness> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "fairness"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.5" ;
  skos:definition "The practice of treating people with equal respect and consideration, and making decisions without bias or unjust discrimination."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Fairness may also involve: a) accounting for unequal barriers and aiming to prevent disadvantages linked to identity or status; b) using transparent and consistent decision-making, so that treatment and opportunities are equitable."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-6> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/equality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/bias>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectionality> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "intersectionality"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.6" ;
  skos:definition "An analytical framework that examines how multiple systems of oppression (such as racism, sexism, classism, ageism, and ableism) overlap and operate together, producing specific forms of disadvantage and exclusion that cannot be understood as the simple sum of different axes of discrimination, but rather as the result of the interaction between them"@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The term was coined by American legal scholar and civil rights activist Kimberlé Williams Crenshaw to critique the single-axis approach traditionally adopted by U.S. courts. and demonstrate that traditional anti-discrimination frameworks often fail to capture the experiences of individuals situated at the intersection of multiple identities."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-6> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/political-intersectionality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/representational-intersectionality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-intersectionality> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectional-discrimination> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/political-intersectionality> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "political intersectionality"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.6.1" ;
  skos:definition "A framework of analysis that challenges interlocked vectors of oppression and the silencing of some identities in social movements as well as institutional arenas."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It advocates for an analysis of a group’s differences through processes of reflexivity and accountability and challenges “identity politics” that look at a social problem (such as racism or sexism) one-sidedly."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectionality> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/meaningful-participation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/minoritised-groups> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/representational-intersectionality> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "representational intersectionality"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.6.2" ;
  skos:definition "A framework of analysis that focuses on how intersecting axes of discrimination shape the way in which the images and representation of marginal groups are constructed."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectionality> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectional-stereotype>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-stereotypes> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-intersectionality> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "structural intersectionality"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.6.3" ;
  skos:definition "A framework of analysis that considers how the intersections of race, gender, class, immigration status, and other axes of inequality shape individuals' access to resources, rights, protection, and opportunities within social, legal, economic, and political institutions."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It highlights how institutional arrangements and public policies often fail to address the specific experiences and needs of individuals who occupy intersecting disadvantaged positions."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectionality> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectional-discrimination> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/minoritised-groups> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "minoritised groups"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.7" ;
  skos:definition "Groups of individuals who are systematically placed in a position of disadvantage or marginalisation through policies, practices, traditions, norms, and dominant narratives that sustain unequal power relations."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Using the term “minoritised” instead of “minority”, makes it clear that being minoritised is about power and equity, not numbers."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-6>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-3>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-7> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectionality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/racism-and-racialisation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/racism-and-racialisation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "racism and racialisation"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.8" ;
  skos:definition "Processes through which hierarchical characteristics and social positions are attributed to individuals or groups on the basis of their origin, appearance, culture or ancestry"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-6> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/ethnocentrism>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectionality> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/segregation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "segregation"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.9" ;
  skos:definition "A condition characterised by an unequal distribution of resources, services, and opportunities available to different social groups, based on several and possibly intersecting axes of discrimination, such as disability, gender, race, social and economic status, and so on."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Segregation may also take spatial forms, when a social group is forced to occupy urban spaces separated from those inhabited by other groups, primarily on the basis of their origin (ethnic background) and social (class) and/or economic status. Segregation is determined — or at least conditioned — by historical and economic processes that intersect with urban, mobility, and welfare policies."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-6> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/institutionalisation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/spatial-justice>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-discrimination> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/stereotypes> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "stereotypes"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.10" ;
  skos:definition "Preconceived opinions and beliefs about individuals or groups, independent of an assessment of the specific conditions of each individual case, and resulting from a prior process of overgeneralisation and oversimplification."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Stereotypes can relate both to the characteristics, attitudes, abilities and social roles that people belonging to a particular social group are assumed to possess (descriptive dimension), and to the characteristics, attitudes, abilities and social roles that these people are expected to possess (prescriptive dimension). In both cases, stereotypes are never neutral, because they create a hierarchy among subjects and contribute to reproducing asymmetries in power relations."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-6> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-stereotypes>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectional-stereotype> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/bias>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/stigma> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-stereotypes> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "gender stereotypes"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.10.1" ;
  skos:definition "Preconception about the attributes, characteristics or roles that women and men should have or perform on the basis of their sex or gender."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/stereotypes> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectional-stereotype> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "intersectional stereotype"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.10.2" ;
  skos:definition "Stereotypes that arise from the convergence of several grounds – for example sex or gender, disability, ethnic origin, age or sexual orientation – and that generate a specific preconception, distinct from the mere sum of each ground taken separately."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "In the case of women with disabilities, they are often ascribed traits such as asexuality, the supposed inability to mother, or dependency, thereby compounding discrimination and making it harder to remedy when each ground is examined in isolation. International law requires combating them: the CRPD recognises women and girls with disabilities as a group exposed to multiple forms of discrimination (art. 6) and requires action against stereotypes, prejudices and harmful practices affecting persons with disabilities (art. 8)."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/stereotypes> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-stereotypes>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectional-discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectionality> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/stigma> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "stigma"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.11" ;
  skos:definition "The structural and societal imposition of negative stereotypes, prejudices, and harmful practices relating to a certain category of persona. These grounds are perceived to distinguish the person or group from other members of society. Stigma can be affixed to a person by society if they differ from cultural norms."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "In the context of women with disabilities, stigma operates as an intersectional barrier where stereotypes about both gender and impairment overlap. This process fosters social exclusion, increases exposure to gender-based violence, and acts as a safeguarding failure that restricts access to justice and protective support networks. Within this human rights framework, tackling stigma is recognised as an essential prerequisite to achieving 'respect for inherent dignity, individual autonomy, and full inclusion in society', as stigma directly drives the multiple and intersectional forms of discrimination and violence faced by girls and women with disabilities"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-6> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/self-stigma> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/stereotypes>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/self-stigma> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "self-stigma"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.11.1" ;
  skos:definition "The process by which individuals internalise negative stereotypes, prejudices, and discriminatory attitudes about a condition or social group, leading to negative beliefs and feelings about themselves."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/stigma> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/shame>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/personal-barriers> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/vulnerability> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "vulnerability"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.12" ;
  skos:definition "Exposure to harm."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-6> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/ontological-vulnerability>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/pathogenic-vulnerability>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/situational-vulnerability> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/ontological-vulnerability> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "ontological vulnerability"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.12.1" ;
  skos:definition "The vulnerability shared by all human beings because we have bodily needs and depend on others throughout our lives."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/vulnerability> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/interdependence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/care> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/pathogenic-vulnerability> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "pathogenic vulnerability"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.12.2" ;
  skos:definition "Vulnerability created or worsened by harmful relationships, violence, unjust social conditions, or measures that leave people with less control over their lives"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/vulnerability> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/situational-vulnerability>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/abuse>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-discrimination> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/situational-vulnerability> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "situational vulnerability"@en ;
  skos:notation "6.12.3" ;
  skos:definition "Vulnerability caused or increased by a person’s particular circumstances."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Due to their disability, gender, and intersecting disadvantages, women with disabilities experience an increased risk of discrimination, violence, limited access to services, and social exclusion, that makes them more susceptible to harm and marginalisation"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/vulnerability> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectionality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/situations-of-risk> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/affection-affectivity> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "affection/affectivity"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.1" ;
  skos:definition "Tender and caring interaction between two or more people that reinforces an emotional tie and constructs a long-lasting bond."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "This intrinsically positive rewarding feeling can occur between partners, friends or family, but it always nurtures emotional safety and self-esteem."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-7> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/care>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/family>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/eroticism> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/body> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "body"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.2" ;
  skos:definition "The biological, embodied, and socially interpreted dimension of a person through which individuals experience the world, participate in social relations, and are classified and evaluated."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It also carries cultural meanings related to sex, femininity, ability, attractiveness, sexuality, and independence. In this sense, bodily characteristics are socially interpreted as markers of sex and as a basis for expectations directed at girls and women, while disability may modify or challenge normative representations of the female body (This approach is consistent with the CRPD perspective, which distinguishes between sex and gender and highlights their interconnections with disability and discrimination). The body can be analysed through bodily characteristics and functions, the experience of one’s own body, its social evaluation and regulation, norms concerning appearance and femininity, experiences of sexuality and reproduction, as well as barriers and practices affecting bodily autonomy."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-7> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sex>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/autonomy> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/eroticism> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "eroticism"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.3" ;
  skos:altLabel "erotism"@en ;
  skos:definition "The totality of practices surrounding sexuality that extend beyond physical coitus, encompassing seduction, arousal, the exploration of pleasure, yearnings, and desire, as well as other psychological, representational, imaginative or artistic dimensions that accompany, complement, and nurture an individual's or common sexuality."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It is the defining element that distinguishes primary reproductive sexuality from the complex human relationship with affective-sexual interactions, whether with oneself or with others."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-7> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/affection-affectivity>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-behaviour>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-rights> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "gender"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.4" ;
  skos:definition "According to the Council of Europe Convention on preventing and combating violence against women and domestic violence (Istanbul Convention), gender may be defined as the “socially constructed roles, behaviours, activities and attributes that a given society considers appropriate for women and men” (art. 3, lett. c)."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "In a different conception, gender may also indicate a person’s self-perception of her identity"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-7> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-gap>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-inequality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-role> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-identity>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sex> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-gap> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "gender gap"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.4.1" ;
  skos:definition "Gender difference/gap is understood as an observable and measurable disparity between women and men or girls and boys in terms of participation, access to resources, rights, benefits, opportunities, or social outcomes."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "A difference does not necessarily constitute inequality, but it may indicate its presence when it is systematic and linked to gender-related norms, roles, and social structures. In the case of persons with disabilities, gender differences should be analysed intersectionally, as gender and disability may jointly shape distinct experiences and social positions. Gender differences can be examined through sex- or gender-based comparisons in areas such as education, employment, income, health, autonomy, safety, care, access to services, and participation in decision-making, while taking the type of disability into account."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-inequality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectionality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectional-discrimination> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-inequality> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "gender inequality"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.4.2" ;
  skos:definition "The socially produced and maintained differentiation of rights, resources, opportunities, power, safety, and recognition based on gender and the social norms and roles associated with it."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "This inequality does not result from gender difference itself, but from institutional and cultural mechanisms that assign women and men different social positions and degrees of agency. In the case of girls and women with disabilities, it has an intersectional character: gender and disability may reinforce one another, creating specific forms of exclusion and discrimination. Gender inequality can be analysed through differences in access to education, employment, income, healthcare and reproductive rights, participation in decision-making, safety, autonomy, support and services, as well as through experiences of stereotyping, violence, and discrimination."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-gap>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/patriarchy>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectional-discrimination> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-role> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "gender role"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.4.3" ;
  skos:definition "The learned socially constructed definitions, expectations, and attitudes socially assigned to individuals based on their perceived gender within cis-heteronormative systems."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "These culturally constructed codes of behaviour dictate how society expects people to dress, speak, act, work, and even love. Gendered roles can perpetuate gender stereotypes, for instance, promoting models of toxic masculinity and submissive femininity. They likewise perpetuate the gender binary male/female silencing non-gender conforming identifications. Gender division not only establishes differences between women and men but also reinforces and perpetuates relationships of inequality. Social learning plays a fundamental role in shaping the expectations, attitudes, and behaviours associated with gender. Through various agents of socialisation — including the family, educational institutions, the media, and peer groups — different activities, responsibilities, and social roles are assigned to men and women. It should be noted that gendered roles interlock with other social ideologies, such as racial biases."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-stereotypes>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-inequality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reproductive-labour> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/girl> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "girl"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.5" ;
  skos:definition "Girls are children and adolescents who identify as female or are socially recognised as such."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The term generally refers to persons below the age of eighteen, although its precise meaning may vary across legal, social, and cultural contexts. Girls are not a homogeneous group: their experiences are shaped by age, disability, ethnicity, socioeconomic position, migration status, sexual orientation, gender identity, and other intersecting conditions."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-7> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/children-with-disabilities>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-identity> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/lgbtiq> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "LGBTIQ+"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.6" ;
  skos:definition "Umbrella term for people whose sexual orientation, gender identity, or sex characteristics differ from conventional heterosexual, cisgender, or binary norms."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-7> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gay-lesbian>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-expression>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-identity>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersex>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/queer>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-orientation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/transgender> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gay-lesbian> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "gay/lesbian"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.6.1" ;
  skos:definition "A person whose affective-sexual orientation is toward people of the same gender."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "“Lesbian” refers specifically to women attracted to women; “gay” may refer specifically to men attracted to men or, more broadly, to homosexual people."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/lgbtiq> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-orientation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-rights> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-expression> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "gender expression"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.6.2" ;
  skos:definition "The external manifestation of a person’s gender, expressed through name, pronouns, clothing, behaviour, voice, or other socially recognised characteristics."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/lgbtiq> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-identity>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-role> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-identity> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "gender identity"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.6.3" ;
  skos:definition "A person’s deeply felt internal and individual experience of gender, which may or may not correspond to the sex assigned at birth (e.g., woman, man, non-binary)."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/lgbtiq> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-expression>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/transgender>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sex> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersex> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "intersex"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.6.4" ;
  skos:definition "An umbrella term describing people born with sex characteristics (such as chromosomes, gonads, hormones, or anatomy) that do not fit typical binary notions of male or female bodies."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Intersex variations are natural forms of human diversity."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/lgbtiq> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/biological-sex>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-identity> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/queer> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "queer"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.6.5" ;
  skos:definition "An umbrella term, sometimes used politically or as a self-identified label, encompassing people whose affective-sexual orientation, gender identity, or gender expression differs from heterosexual and/or cisgender norms."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Its meaning may vary across contexts."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/lgbtiq> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-orientation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-identity> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-orientation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "sexual orientation"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.6.6" ;
  skos:definition "A person’s capacity for affective, emotional, romantic and/or sexual attraction to people of a particular gender or more than one gender."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/lgbtiq> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gay-lesbian>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-identity>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-behaviour> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/transgender> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "transgender"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.6.7" ;
  skos:definition "A person whose gender identity differs from the sex assigned at birth."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The term includes, for example, trans women, trans men, and some non-binary people."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/lgbtiq> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-identity>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/legal-sex>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-expression> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/male-dominance> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "male dominance"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.7" ;
  skos:definition "A conceptual label to characterise the unequal power relations between men as a group and women as a group."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Rooted in Western thought since Ancient Greece, male dominance has historically justified the exclusion of women from the public sphere and the elevation of masculine traits over feminine ones, often by claiming that such social hierarchies are a natural and desirable consequence of biological differences. Therefore, it refers to a social system in which power is systematically distributed in favour of men, and where what counts as neutral, objective, or universal is in fact defined by male standards. Gender differences thus emerge not as natural facts, but as outcomes of this asymmetrical distribution of power."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-7> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/patriarchy>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-inequality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/misogyny> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/misogyny> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "misogyny"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.8" ;
  skos:definition "A system of practices, norms, and institutional arrangements that sustains gendered domination by regulating, punishing, excluding, or devaluing women and feminised subjects."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Contemporary intersectional and decolonial approaches emphasise that misogyny operates differently across race, class, sexuality, nationality, and colonial histories."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-7> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/patriarchy>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/male-dominance>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-based-violence> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/patriarchy> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "patriarchy"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.9" ;
  skos:definition "A system of social organisation based on asymmetrical gender relations and power structures that shape both family relations and society more broadly, privileging men and systematically producing women’s subordination, discrimination, and oppression while limiting their autonomy, opportunities, and enjoyment of rights in private and public life."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-7> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/male-dominance>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-inequality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-role> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sex> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "sex"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.10" ;
  skos:definition "Classification of a person as female, male, or another category, based on bodily characteristics (biological sex) or on their designation in official records (legal sex)."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-7> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/biological-sex>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/legal-sex> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/biological-sex> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "biological sex"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.10.1" ;
  skos:definition "The multidimensional biological characteristics used to describe and classify human bodies, including chromosomes, gonads, hormones, reproductive anatomy, external genitalia, physiology, and secondary sexual characteristics."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "These features often align in patterns commonly classified as female or male, but they do not always do so. Variations in sex characteristics, including chromosomal, hormonal, gonadal, or anatomical variations, may fall under the broad category of intersex variations and demonstrate that biological sex cannot always be reduced to a strictly binary classification. Sex is usually assigned at birth, primarily on the basis of external genital characteristics, and may subsequently be recorded as a legal or administrative status. Although sex classification draws on biological features, the criteria through which those features are selected, interpreted, and registered are also shaped by medical, legal, social, and cultural practices. Sex and gender identity are related but analytically distinct concepts. A person’s sex characteristics or assigned sex may not correspond to their gender identity."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sex> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersex>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-identity> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/legal-sex> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "legal sex"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.10.2" ;
  skos:definition "The sex category officially recorded by public authorities in civil status registers, identity documents or other official records."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It is generally assigned at birth on the basis of observed sex characteristics and constitutes a legal status that may subsequently differ from a person’s biological characteristics or gender identity. Depending on the relevant national legal framework, legal sex may be amended through a formal procedure for legal recognition. The categories that may be officially recorded and the requirements for such recognition vary across legal systems; surgical modification of sex characteristics is not necessarily required. In research, legal and policy contexts, legal sex should be distinguished from sex assigned at birth and gender identity, particularly where official documentation, access to rights or services, or data collection depend on the legally recognised sex marker"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sex> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-identity> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-behaviour> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "sexual behaviour"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.11" ;
  skos:definition "It is an act of connection with oneself or another including intimacy, pleasure, bonding and physical gratification."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Beyond its reproductive purpose, sex can be the physical manifestation of love, desire, passion and fantasies"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-7> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/eroticism> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/woman> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "woman"@en ;
  skos:notation "7.12" ;
  skos:definition "For the purposes of this dictionary, the term includes both cisgender women - persons assigned female at birth whose gender identity is female - and transgender women (persons whose gender identity is female and does not correspond to the sex assigned at birth). This inclusive use does not make sex, legal sex and gender identity interchangeable."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Women do not constitute a homogeneous group: their experiences, opportunities and exposure to discrimination may vary according to disability, age, racialisation, ethnic or cultural minority status, religion, migration status, socio-economic position and other intersecting factors"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-7> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sex>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-identity>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectionality> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/coercion-in-mental-health-care> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "coercion in mental health care"@en ;
  skos:notation "8.1" ;
  skos:definition "The use of involuntary admission, treatment, medication, seclusion or restraint without free and informed consent."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-8>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-13> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/free-and-informed-consent>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/institutional-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/retraumatisation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/diagnostic-overshadowing> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "diagnostic overshadowing"@en ;
  skos:notation "8.2" ;
  skos:definition "The tendency to attribute physical or emotional symptoms, or accounts of violence, to a disability or mental health diagnosis without investigating them properly."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-8> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/ableism>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/medicalisation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/disability> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/free-and-informed-consent> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "free and informed consent"@en ;
  skos:notation "8.3" ;
  skos:altLabel "consent"@en ;
  skos:definition "A person's voluntary acceptance of a medical, care-related or research intervention, on the basis of sufficient, understandable and accessible information – nature, risks, benefits and alternatives – and free from coercion, deception or undue influence; it also includes the right to refuse or withdraw it."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The CRPD requires it in health care (art. 25), prohibits medical or scientific experiments without free consent (art. 15) and protects personal integrity (art. 17). In relation to legal capacity (art. 12), General Comment No. 1 (2014) rejects substitute decision-making and calls for support so that the decision expresses the person's will and preferences. It is the guarantee that distinguishes legitimate intervention from practices such as forced sterilisation or forced treatment."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-8>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-10> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/legal-capacity>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/will-and-preferences>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/forced-sterilisation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reproductive-rights>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support-in-decision-making> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/medicalisation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "medicalisation"@en ;
  skos:notation "8.4" ;
  skos:definition "The reframing of social or life experiences as exclusively medical problems, obscuring factors such as violence, racism, precarity or isolation."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-8> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/medical-model-of-disability>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-determinants-of-mental-health>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/diagnostic-overshadowing> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/mental-health> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "mental health"@en ;
  skos:notation "8.5" ;
  skos:definition "A state of mental well-being that enables people to cope with the stresses of life, realise their abilities, learn well and work well, and contribute to their community."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It has intrinsic and instrumental value and is a basic human right"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-8> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-determinants-of-mental-health> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/recovery>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/trauma> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-determinants-of-mental-health> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "social determinants of mental health"@en ;
  skos:notation "8.5.1" ;
  skos:definition "The social, economic, cultural and political conditions that influence mental health, including housing, income, education, employment, violence, discrimination and support networks."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/mental-health> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/poverty>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support-network> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/recovery> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "recovery"@en ;
  skos:notation "8.6" ;
  skos:definition "A personal process of building a meaningful life with rights, relationships and decision-making power, whether or not symptoms persist."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-8> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/empowerment>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/supported-autonomy>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/mental-health> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/resilience> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "resilience"@en ;
  skos:notation "8.7" ;
  skos:definition "The process of positive adaptation to adversity, trauma, tragedy, threats, or significant sources of stress, such as family and relationship problems, serious health issues, or work and financial stress."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "While resilience involves recovering these difficult experiences, it can also lead to profound personal growth."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-8> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/trauma>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/recovery>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support-network> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/retraumatisation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "retraumatisation"@en ;
  skos:notation "8.8" ;
  skos:definition "The reactivation or worsening of trauma as a result of coercive, invasive, unpredictable or insensitive practices."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-8> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/trauma>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/trauma-informed-care>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/revictimisation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/shame> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "shame"@en ;
  skos:notation "8.9" ;
  skos:definition "An intensely painful feeling or experience of believing we are flawed and therefore unworthy of acceptance and belonging."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Shame is a psycho-social-cultural construct. The psychological component relates to the participants’ emphasis on the emotions, thoughts, and behaviours of self. The social component relates to the way people experience shame in an interpersonal context that is inextricably tied to relationships and connection. The cultural component points to the very prevalent role of cultural expectations and the relationship between shame and the real or perceived failure of meeting cultural expectations."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-8> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/self-stigma>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/stigma>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/trauma> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/trauma> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "trauma"@en ;
  skos:notation "8.10" ;
  skos:definition "Trauma is defined as a deeply disturbing or overwhelming experience that severely compromises a group or a person's sense of safety, often resulting in lasting negative effects on their mental, physical, and emotional well-being."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It is entirely subjective, meaning how an individual processes the event determines if it becomes traumatic. Trauma results in significant fear, helplessness, confusion, or other disruptive feelings intense enough to have a long-lasting negative effect on a group or person’s attitudes, behaviour, and other aspects of functioning. Traumatic events include those caused by human behaviour (e.g., rape, war, industrial accidents) as well as natural disasters (e.g., earthquakes) and often challenge an individual’s view of the world as a just, safe, and predictable place."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-8> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/trauma-informed-care> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/retraumatisation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/mental-health> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/trauma-informed-care> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "trauma-informed care"@en ;
  skos:notation "8.10.1" ;
  skos:definition "Care that recognises the effects of trauma, avoids blame and seeks to ensure safety, trust, choice, collaboration and control for the person receiving support."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/trauma> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/retraumatisation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/person-centred-support> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/credibility--qualitative-research> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "credibility (qualitative research)"@en ;
  skos:notation "9.1" ;
  skos:definition "Credibility refers to the degree to which qualitative research findings reflect participants' reality and are an accurate interpretation of their original views."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "This concept was introduced into qualitative methodology by Egon Guba and Yvonne Lincoln (1985) as part of the broader concept of trustworthiness. It is the qualitative equivalent of internal validity used in quantitative research. Instead of seeking objective truth through statistical measures, researchers strive to demonstrate that their conclusions are credible, consistent, and rooted in empirical evidence. The most important strategies for increasing credibility include: Triangulation: using multiple data sources, methods, theories, or researchers. Member checking: presenting results or transcripts to participants to confirm whether their intentions have been correctly understood. Prolonged engagement: spending an appropriate amount of time in the research environment. Reflexivity: documenting the researcher's own assumptions and biases that may influence interpretation."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-9> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/participatory-research>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/situated-knowledge>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/bias> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/heterodox-perspective-methodology> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "heterodox perspective/methodology"@en ;
  skos:notation "9.2" ;
  skos:definition "Approaches that depart from or critically challenge dominant theories, methods, categories, or institutionalised forms of knowledge."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "They broaden the range of questions, sources, and interpretive frameworks considered legitimate and may bring into view experiences or forms of knowledge marginalised by prevailing paradigms."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-9> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/situated-knowledge>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/decolonial-studies>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/transformative-approach> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusive-research> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "inclusive research"@en ;
  skos:notation "9.3" ;
  skos:definition "A specific form of participatory research characterised by the substantive involvement of people with intellectual disabilities in defining, conducting and using research"@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The mere participation of these individuals in a study does not automatically make participatory research inclusive. Inclusive research requires researchers and other stakeholders to be willing to listen to, learn from, and share knowledge with the people with intellectual disabilities involved. Research may be considered inclusive when: (1) those involved influence the research agenda; (2) they participate in decision-making throughout the research process; (3) they contribute to the production and interpretation of data; (4) they benefit in some way from the research; and (5) they are recognised as legitimate producers of knowledge. The core of inclusive research does not lie in the use of a particular technique or methodology, but rather in the democratisation of the knowledge and power relations that shape the research process."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-9> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/emancipatory-research>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/participatory-research> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/meaningful-participation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/emancipatory-research> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "emancipatory research"@en ;
  skos:notation "9.3.1" ;
  skos:definition "Research that is controlled by those directly affected, with the aim of empowering them through both the research process and its outcomes."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Emancipatory research is usually defined in political terms and has a stronger political orientation. It is described as a means of challenging social oppression. The oppression experienced by people with disabilities requires a transformation in the nature of research relationships. Researchers should place themselves at the service of people with disabilities. Furthermore, people with disabilities should have control over the resources involved in the research process, a key distinction between emancipatory and participatory research. The research process is therefore inherently politicised, since its purpose extends beyond knowledge production to the transformation of unequal social relations and the empowerment of disabled people."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusive-research> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/participatory-research>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/empowerment>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/transformative-approach> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/participatory-research> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "participatory research"@en ;
  skos:notation "9.3.2" ;
  skos:altLabel "participatory action research"@en ;
  skos:definition "Research that involves democratic relationships in the production of knowledge, incorporating participants’ everyday knowledge, as well as in problem-solving."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It is a research process that involves those being researched in decision-making and in the conduct of the research itself, including project planning, research design, data collection, data analysis, dissemination, and the application of findings. This highlights the fact that people may participate at different stages throughout the research process."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusive-research> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/emancipatory-research>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/meaningful-participation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/situated-knowledge> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "situated knowledge"@en ;
  skos:notation "9.4" ;
  skos:definition "Knowledge produced from a particular social, historical, embodied, and institutional position."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The concept of situated knowledge rejects the idea of a fully neutral or disembodied standpoint and requires attention to how the position of the knower shapes what can be perceived, understood, and represented. Situatedness does not imply that all claims are equally valid, but that knowledge claims should be assessed with awareness of their conditions, limits, and relations of power."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-9> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/heterodox-perspective-methodology>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectionality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/credibility--qualitative-research> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/transformative-approach> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "transformative approach"@en ;
  skos:notation "9.5" ;
  skos:definition "An approach that seeks to produce lasting and structural change rather than merely addressing the immediate effects of a problem."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It examines and challenges the underlying norms, institutions, practices, and power relations that generate or reproduce inequality, exclusion, harm, or disadvantage. Its aim is not simply to include marginalised persons within existing arrangements, but to modify those arrangements so that they become more equitable, participatory, and responsive to diverse experiences and needs."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-9> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/emancipatory-research>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/human-rights-based-approach> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/access-to-justice> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "access to justice"@en ;
  skos:notation "10.1" ;
  skos:definition "The right and real possibility of every person to turn to the courts and to other dispute-resolution mechanisms, and to participate in them effectively and on an equal basis, whether as a party, witness, victim or in any other capacity."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It is not exhausted by formal judicial protection: it requires removing the economic, physical, communication and attitudinal barriers that impede it. For persons with disabilities, the CRPD guarantees it in art. 13, which requires procedural accommodations that are age- and gender-appropriate, accessibility and training for those working in the administration of justice; unlike reasonable accommodation, procedural accommodations are not subject to the disproportionate-burden limit. It also has a gender dimension – justice free from stereotypes for women victims of violence – and a childhood dimension – child-friendly justice for children and adolescents."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-10> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/mediation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reasonable-accommodation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/procedural-accommodation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/victim>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/legal-capacity> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accountability> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "accountability"@en ;
  skos:notation "10.2" ;
  skos:definition "The obligation of an individual, institution, or organisation to explain and justify its decisions and actions, to be subject to scrutiny, and to accept responsibility for their consequences."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-10> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/human-rights-based-approach>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/safeguards>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accountability--robotics> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/human-rights-based-approach> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "human rights-based approach"@en ;
  skos:notation "10.3" ;
  skos:definition "An approach that recognises women and girls as rights-holders and institutions as duty-bearers responsible for respecting, protecting and fulfilling those rights."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-10> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/equality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accountability>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/meaningful-participation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/legal-capacity> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "legal capacity"@en ;
  skos:notation "10.4" ;
  skos:definition "Recognition of every person as a holder of rights and as capable of exercising them on their own."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-10> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/guardianship>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/will-and-preferences> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/personal-assistance>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support-in-decision-making> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/guardianship> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "guardianship"@en ;
  skos:notation "10.4.1" ;
  skos:definition "A legal institution whereby a third party is appointed to take decisions in place of a person considered incapable of taking them on their own."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "With regard to adults with disabilities, the CRPD rejects this model of substitution of the will: art. 12 recognises their legal capacity on an equal basis and requires replacing it with support measures guided by the person's will and preferences, with safeguards against abuse, conflict of interest and undue influence. General Comment No. 1 (2014) makes clear that substitute decision-making regimes must be abolished. This sense must not be confused with the guardianship of children who lack parents exercising parental responsibility, which belongs to the field of child protection."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/legal-capacity> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support-in-decision-making>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/safeguards>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/alternative-care> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/will-and-preferences> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "will and preferences"@en ;
  skos:notation "10.4.2" ;
  skos:definition "The standard that must guide the exercise of legal capacity and any support in decision-making: what the person wants and prefers, not what others consider objectively best for them."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The CRPD, as interpreted by General Comment No. 1 (2014), replaces the \"best interests\" model as applied to adults with respect for their will and preferences. Where, after serious efforts, these cannot be determined, the best interpretation of the person's will and preferences must apply, never a substitute decision. It is a central standard against paternalism, substitute guardianship and infantilisation."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/legal-capacity> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support-in-decision-making>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/safeguards>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/free-and-informed-consent>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/infantilisation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/mediation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "mediation"@en ;
  skos:notation "10.5" ;
  skos:definition "A voluntary and confidential procedure in which an impartial third party, with no decision-making power, helps the parties to a dispute to reach an agreement by themselves."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Under the CRPD, mediation must be accessible and have the procedural accommodations and supports needed for the person with a disability to participate on an equal basis, with their legal capacity recognised and without substitution of their will (arts. 9, 12 and 13). It also has an essential limit: art. 48 of the Istanbul Convention prohibits mandatory mediation in cases of violence against women or domestic violence, a caution that is especially relevant where disability and dependency coincide."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-10> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/family-mediation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/legal-mediation> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/access-to-justice>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/procedural-accommodation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/violence-against-women> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/family-mediation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "family mediation"@en ;
  skos:notation "10.5.1" ;
  skos:definition "The application of mediation to family disputes: an impartial third party, with no decision-making power, helps family members to reach agreements by themselves on their crisis or reorganisation."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Where children or adolescents are involved, the outcome must be governed by their best interests and must respect their right to be heard. The requirements of accessibility, accommodation and support apply to it, as does the limit on mandatory mediation in contexts of violence."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/mediation> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/best-interests-of-the-child>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/right-of-the-child-to-be-heard>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/child-custody>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/violence-against-women> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/legal-mediation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "legal mediation"@en ;
  skos:notation "10.5.2" ;
  skos:definition "A structured and generally confidential process in which an impartial third party assists the parties to a legal dispute in communicating, identifying their interests, exploring possible solutions, and, where possible, reaching a voluntary agreement."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The mediator does not impose a binding decision but facilitates negotiation and helps the parties retain control over the outcome. Legal mediation may take place before or during judicial proceedings and is shaped by the applicable legal and institutional framework."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/mediation> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/access-to-justice>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/family-mediation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/procedural-accommodation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "procedural accommodation"@en ;
  skos:notation "10.6" ;
  skos:definition "The necessary modifications and adjustments in judicial and administrative proceedings so that persons with disabilities can take part in them on an equal basis, in any capacity (party, witness, victim, juror, etc.)."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The CRPD requires them in art. 13 as a condition of access to justice: they include, for instance, accessible formats, plain language, interpreters, court facilitators, the adaptation of timeframes and spaces, or assistance with communication. Unlike reasonable accommodation, they are not subject to the disproportionate-burden limit: they can never be denied on grounds of cost or excessive effort. They must also be age- and gender-appropriate."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-10> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/access-to-justice>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reasonable-accommodation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/legal-capacity>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/mediation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reasonable-accommodation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "reasonable accommodation"@en ;
  skos:notation "10.7" ;
  skos:definition "Necessary and appropriate modifications and adjustments which, without imposing a disproportionate or undue burden, are made in a particular case to enable a person with a disability to exercise their fundamental rights and freedoms on an equal basis."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It is an individualised obligation of immediate application. It is distinct from accessibility and universal design, which are general and anticipatory duties, and from procedural accommodations in the field of justice (art. 13), which are not subject to the same disproportionate-burden limit. The CRPD defines it in art. 2 and treats its denial as a form of discrimination, requiring States to ensure it (art. 5.3). It is a cross-cutting guarantee of the equal exercise of rights and has, in EU law, a clear positive anchor in the field of employment (Directive 2000/78/EC, art. 5; CJEU, HK Danmark, 2013)."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-10> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reasonable-accommodation-in-employment--employment-law> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accessibility>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/universal-design>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/procedural-accommodation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reasonable-accommodation-in-employment--employment-law> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "reasonable accommodation in employment (employment law)"@en ;
  skos:notation "10.7.1" ;
  skos:definition "Reasonable accommodation is the obligation of an employer to take appropriate and necessary measures to enable a person with a disability to access, participate in, or advance in employment, or to undergo training on an equal basis with others, unless such measures would impose a disproportionate burden on the employer considering factors such as financial cost, size and resources of the undertaking, and availability of public support."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "These measures may include adjustments to working conditions, job duties, workplace organisation, equipment, working hours, or other practical changes that eliminate barriers and ensure effective participation."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reasonable-accommodation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-14> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/employment-discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusion--employment> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/safeguards> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "safeguards"@en ;
  skos:notation "10.8" ;
  skos:definition "Guarantees that must accompany any measure relating to the exercise of legal capacity in order to prevent abuse."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Under art. 12.4 CRPD, they must ensure that support respects the person's rights, will and preferences, is free from conflict of interest and undue influence, is proportionate and tailored to the person's circumstances, applies for the shortest possible time and is subject to regular review by a competent, independent and impartial authority or judicial body. They are not a control over the person, but protection against the misuse of support."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-10> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/legal-capacity>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support-in-decision-making>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/will-and-preferences>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/guardianship> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/situations-of-risk> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "situations of risk"@en ;
  skos:notation "10.9" ;
  skos:definition "Contexts in which a person's life, safety, integrity or effective exercise of rights may be seriously threatened."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The expression shifts the focus away from a supposed intrinsic vulnerability of the person towards the external conditions that increase exposure to harm — armed conflict, humanitarian emergencies, natural disasters, displacement, institutionalisation or barriers to accessing information and services: it is a category centred on the context, not on a fixed identity. Art. 11 CRPD requires States to ensure the protection and safety of persons with disabilities in such situations, and the OHCHR study on that provision interprets it from a human rights approach, moving beyond welfare-based or medical readings. The response must be inclusive and accessible at every stage – prevention, evacuation, rescue, care and recovery –, with understandable information, the provision of supports and the participation of the persons affected themselves, including women and children with disabilities."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-10> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accessibility>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reasonable-accommodation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/independent-living-and-inclusion-in-the-community>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/violence-against-women>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/best-interests-of-the-child> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/victim> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "victim"@en ;
  skos:notation "10.10" ;
  skos:definition "A person who, individually or collectively, has suffered harm – physical, psychological, emotional or economic, or an impairment of their fundamental rights – as a result of a crime or an abuse of power, regardless of whether the perpetrator is identified or convicted."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Victim status entails rights: to information, protection, support, participation in proceedings and reparation, as well as to be free from revictimisation or secondary victimisation, i.e. the additional harm caused by the system itself. In gender-based violence it also covers indirect victims, such as the children. Victims with disabilities require procedural accommodations and accessibility to exercise those rights on an equal basis."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-10> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/revictimisation> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/access-to-justice>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/violence-against-women>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/vicarious-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/procedural-accommodation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/revictimisation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "revictimisation"@en ;
  skos:notation "10.10.1" ;
  skos:altLabel "revictimization"@en ;
  skos:definition "Additional harm caused when institutions or professionals question, blame or expose a woman, or require her to recount her experience of violence unnecessarily."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "This situation is also referred to as secondary victimisation."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/victim> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/retraumatisation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/institutional-violence> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accessible-comprehensive-sexuality-education> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "accessible comprehensive sexuality education"@en ;
  skos:notation "11.1" ;
  skos:definition "Age-appropriate and accessible education about the body, relationships, consent, pleasure, diversity, the prevention of violence, and sexual and reproductive rights."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-11> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-rights>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reproductive-rights>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/free-and-informed-consent> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/contraception> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "contraception"@en ;
  skos:notation "11.2" ;
  skos:definition "A set of reversible or permanent methods, measures, and practices aimed at preventing pregnancy, the choice of which should be based on reliable information, voluntariness, and informed consent."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "WHO considers access to contraception an element of sexual and reproductive health and of the right to decide freely and responsibly on the number and spacing of children. In the case of girls and women with disabilities, contraception should also be analysed in the context of cultural norms concerning sexuality, gender, and motherhood, as well as stereotypes portraying them as asexual, incapable of parenthood, or in need of reproductive control. Research identifies communication, architectural, and institutional barriers, as well as staff attitudes, that may limit genuine choice of contraceptive methods. Contraception can be analysed through knowledge of and access to methods, their use, freedom of choice, quality of counselling, availability of services, consent and decision-making autonomy, and experiences of pressure, refusal, or coercion."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-11> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reproductive-rights>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/free-and-informed-consent>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/forced-sterilisation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/forced-sterilisation> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "forced sterilisation"@en ;
  skos:notation "11.3" ;
  skos:definition "An intervention that removes reproductive capacity without free, prior and informed consent."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It constitutes a human rights violation and a form of violence and discrimination that disproportionately affects women and girls with disabilities. International law prohibits it because it harms personal integrity, fertility and the right to health care based on informed consent, and European case law has condemned it as inhuman or degrading treatment."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-11> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reproductive-rights>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/legal-capacity>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/violence-against-women>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/free-and-informed-consent> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gynaecological-care> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "gynaecological care"@en ;
  skos:notation "11.4" ;
  skos:definition "Preventive, diagnostic, therapeutic, and counselling services related to the gynaecological and reproductive health of women and girls, provided with respect for autonomy, privacy, informed consent, and the right to information."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "For women and girls with disabilities, accessibility also includes the physical, communication, and informational adaptation of services to individual needs, free from stereotyping and discrimination. Research indicates that infrastructural, organizational, and communication barriers, as well as interactions with healthcare professionals, may limit the quality of and effective access to care. Gynaecological care can be analysed through access to prevention, consultations, diagnostics and treatment, accessibility of facilities and equipment, communication with healthcare staff, respect for consent and privacy, and experiences of barriers and discrimination."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-11> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accessibility>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/free-and-informed-consent>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reproductive-rights>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/obstetric-violence> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/motherhood> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "motherhood"@en ;
  skos:notation "11.5" ;
  skos:definition "A socially and culturally constructed status, identity, and set of practices related to giving birth, raising and caring for a child, as well as to assigning women responsibility for the child’s well-being."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It is not merely a consequence of biological childbirth, but a relationship and social role shaped by gender norms, ideals of the “good mother,” care arrangements, family relations, public policies, and access to resources and support. Motherhood includes both women’s experience and self-identification as mothers, as well as care practices and social expectations concerning who should provide care and how. It is also linked to reproductive and family rights, as cultural and institutional norms may limit women’s recognition as potential or competent mothers. Operationally, motherhood can be analysed through pathways to motherhood, reproductive aspirations and decisions, the organisation of everyday care, maternal identity and meanings, social expectations, access to support and services, and the ability to exercise parental rights. This approach is consistent with Article 23 of the CRPD and General Comment No. 3, which stress the equal right of persons with disabilities to family life and parenthood and the duty to provide appropriate support."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-11>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-2> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/parental-responsibility>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/right-to-marry-and-to-found-a-family>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/care-work> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reproductive-rights> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "reproductive rights"@en ;
  skos:notation "11.6" ;
  skos:definition "The right of every person to decide freely and responsibly on the number, spacing and timing of their children, to have the information, education and means to do so, and to attain the highest possible standard of sexual and reproductive health, free from discrimination, coercion and violence."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Their contemporary international formulation was shaped above all in Cairo (1994) and has a clear treaty anchor in art. 16.1 (e) of CEDAW. The CRPD recognises them on an equal basis: deciding on offspring and retaining fertility (art. 23) and access to sexual and reproductive health on the basis of free and informed consent (art. 25), with support in decision-making where necessary and never through substitution of the will (art. 12). Their violation – such as forced or non-consensual sterilisation, contraception or abortion – is a form of violence that disproportionately affects women and girls with disabilities."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-11> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/forced-sterilisation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/legal-capacity>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/free-and-informed-consent>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/contraception>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/motherhood> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-rights> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "sexual rights"@en ;
  skos:notation "11.7" ;
  skos:definition "The right of every person to live their sexuality and to make decisions about their body, their relationships and their intimate life freely and safely, free from discrimination, coercion and violence, and with access to the information, education and health services they need."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "No autonomous treaty defines them; they derive from the rights to equality, privacy, personal integrity, health and autonomy, and have been shaped in the contemporary international development of women's human rights, especially from Cairo (1994) and the Beijing Platform for Action (1995). The CRPD requires that persons with disabilities be recognised as holders of these rights on an equal basis: it protects their private life (art. 22) and their personal and family relationships (art. 23), and guarantees their access to sexual and reproductive health on the basis of free and informed consent (art. 25), in connection with legal capacity and support in decision-making (art. 12)."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-11> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sex-assistant> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reproductive-rights>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/free-and-informed-consent>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/legal-capacity>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accessible-comprehensive-sexuality-education>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-violence> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sex-assistant> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "sex assistant"@en ;
  skos:notation "11.7.1" ;
  skos:definition "A specialised practitioner who assists people with disabilities in accessing their right to sexual health."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "This specifically trained professional facilitates sexual expression, body exploration, and physical pleasure for individuals or couples who face physical, cognitive, or psychological barriers to experiencing their own sexuality, helping to fulfil their intimate and affective needs, while reinforcing the sexual autonomy, self-knowledge and emotional safety."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-rights> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/eroticism>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/free-and-informed-consent> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accountability--robotics> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "accountability (robotics)"@en ;
  skos:notation "12.1" ;
  skos:definition "The obligation of operators, including automated systems and their developers to remain transparent and responsible for their decisions and outcomes, particularly where technical limits might otherwise obscure decision-making processes."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "In the EU, when applied to robotics and AI, it is closely linked to legal frameworks like the GDPR and the AI Act, which mandate fairness and the ability to contest or explain algorithmic decisions."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-12> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accountability> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/assistive-technology--robotics> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "assistive technology (robotics)"@en ;
  skos:notation "12.2" ;
  skos:definition "Wearable devices (such as exoskeletons or exosuits) or assistive devices (such as crutches, walkers and aids), designed to augment, enable, assist, or enhance physical activity through direct mechanical interaction with the human body."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "These technologies are often used to compensate for physical limitations or to aid in motor re-education."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-12> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accessibility>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/humanrobot-interaction--robotics>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/usability> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/exclusion--robotics> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "exclusion (robotics)"@en ;
  skos:notation "12.3" ;
  skos:definition "The systematic process or design failure that prevents specific groups – often non-paradigmatic subjects – from accessing or benefiting from a technology."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "An example of exclusion by design of physical interfaces is the use of restrictive physical parameters (like minimum height requirements in exoskeletons) that can exclude significant portions of the population, such as 37% of women."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-12> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/non-paradigmatic-subject>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/universal-design>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/usability> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/humanrobot-interaction--robotics> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "human–robot interaction (robotics)"@en ;
  skos:notation "12.4" ;
  skos:definition "A multidisciplinary field that studies the exchanges between people and robots, encompassing physical interaction, safety protocols, and psychological factors such as trust, engagement, and the perception of the robot by the human user."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It can be differentiated into Physical HRI if the interaction is based on a force exchange between a robot and a human (worn exoskeleton, assistive pedals…) or Cognitive HRI (perception of the robot by the human or of the human by the robot)."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-12> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/assistive-technology--robotics>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/operational-autonomy--robotics>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accountability--robotics> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/operational-autonomy--robotics> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "operational autonomy (robotics)"@en ;
  skos:notation "12.5" ;
  skos:definition "The capacity to operate or make decisions without direct external intervention."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "In the context of robotics is the capacity of a system to operate without direct human supervision. It generally refers to the capacity of a system to achieve its stated aim without human intervention and compensating for external influences and interactions."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-12> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/humanrobot-interaction--robotics>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accountability--robotics> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/usability> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "usability"@en ;
  skos:notation "12.6" ;
  skos:definition "The extent to which a product can be used by specified users to achieve specific goals with effectiveness, efficiency, and satisfaction."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Usability aim is to avoid exclusion. True usability requires a user-centric development process that includes public debate and the active involvement of representative user groups."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-12> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/usability-assessment> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accessibility>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/meaningful-participation> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/usability-assessment> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "usability assessment"@en ;
  skos:notation "12.6.1" ;
  skos:definition "The systematic evaluation of systems from functional, user experience, and methodological perspectives to identify barriers or harmful \"dark patterns\" in design"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/usability> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/exclusion--robotics>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/participatory-research> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/abuse> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "abuse"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.1" ;
  skos:definition "Conduct through which power, authority, trust, or vulnerability is exploited in a manner that harms, controls, humiliates, or deprives another person of autonomy."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-13> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/aggression>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/caregiver-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/autonomy> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/aggression> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "aggression"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.2" ;
  skos:definition "Any behaviour directed toward another individual that is carried out with the proximate (immediate) intent to cause harm."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-13> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/microaggression> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/abuse>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/physical-violence> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/microaggression> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "microaggression"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.2.1" ;
  skos:definition "An everyday comment, action or gesture that conveys contempt, inferiority or exclusion towards a person belonging to a minoritised group."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/aggression> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/minoritised-groups>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/stereotypes>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/caregiver-violence> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "caregiver violence"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.3" ;
  skos:definition "Abuse, neglect, exploitation or control by family members, professionals or other people responsible for providing support."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-13>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-1> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/caregiver>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/abuse>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/withdrawal-of-support> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/domestic-violence> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "domestic violence"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.4" ;
  skos:definition "Any act of physical, sexual, psychological, or economic violence occurring within a family or domestic unit, including violence between parents and children, between siblings, between other family members, or between current or former spouses or partners, regardless of whether the perpetrator shares or has shared a residence with the victim."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-13> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-based-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/psychological-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/economic-violence> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-based-violence> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "gender-based violence"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.5" ;
  skos:definition "Any harmful act directed against a person because of their gender, gender identity, or gender expression, or which disproportionately affects people of a particular gender."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It may take physical, sexual, psychological, economic, or digital forms and is rooted in unequal power relations and harmful gender norms. It can affect anyone but disproportionately impacts women and girls."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-13> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/violence-against-women>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-inequality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-stereotypes> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/harassment> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "harassment"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.6" ;
  skos:definition "An unwanted conduct related to a protected ground that violates the dignity of a person and creates an intimidating, hostile, or offensive environment. This broadens the notion of discrimination to cover harm in both tangible (employment, housing) and intangible (dignitary) dimensions."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Harassment can occur in different contexts (work, family, education). It is recognised in many legal systems (e.g., EU Directive 2000/78/EC) as a form of discrimination."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-13>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-6> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-harassment> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/employment-discrimination> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-harassment> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "sexual harassment"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.6.1" ;
  skos:definition "Any form of unwanted verbal, non-verbal or physical conduct of a sexual nature occurs, with the purpose or effect of violating the dignity of a person, in particular when creating an intimidating, hostile, degrading, humiliating or offensive environment."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Sexual harassment is often perpetrated in contexts involving power asymmetries, promises of benefits, or threats of retaliation and it is both a form of discrimination and a form of violence. What counts as legally relevant sexual harassment can vary from country to country; key factors are whether the affected individual considers the act as unwanted and whether they can distance themselves without fear of reprisals."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/harassment> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-based-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-violence> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/hate> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "hate"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.7" ;
  skos:definition "A negative emotional attitude characterised by a strong hostility towards others."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Root causes of hate include fear of the unknown, perceived threats to one’s identity or resources, and deeply ingrained \"us-versus-them\" binary. Hate-related emotions combine disgust, contempt, and anger. In addition, hate is often regarded as the fundamental opposite of love, but some studies underline its spectrum, that is, individuals have the ability to change these emotions. Unlike fleeting anger, hate tends to be more enduring and deeply rooted. In psychology, self-hate can be the result of internalised fears and trauma. Hate is socially constructed and can trigger hate actions and even hate crimes."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-13>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-6> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/hate-crime>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/hate-speech> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/stereotypes> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/hate-crime> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "hate crime"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.7.1" ;
  skos:definition "Targeted violent actions and discourses motivated by the perpetrator's biases against a victim's identities, including characteristics such as race, religion, sexual orientation, disability, ethnicity, gender, and sexual orientation."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/hate> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/hate-speech>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/bias> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/hate-speech> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "hate speech"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.7.2" ;
  skos:definition "Disparaging expressions directed at individuals or groups based on specific social characteristics, which can take many forms – written, non-verbal, visual, artistic, etc. – while being disseminated through any medium, including the Internet, print materials, radio, or television."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Hate speech not only undermines the dignity of the individuals and groups targeted but also violates the fundamental principles of peaceful coexistence and mutual respect that underpin democratic societies. Its negative effects extend beyond the social and personal spheres, having a troubling impact on the language-learning process. The consequences of hate speech are multifaceted and far-reaching. Research has shown that hate speech has significant psychological and emotional consequences for victims, affecting their self-esteem, self-perception, and interactions with their environment."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/hate> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/hate-crime>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/digital-violence> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/violence-against-women> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "violence against women"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.8" ;
  skos:definition "Any act of gender-based violence that causes or is likely to cause women physical, sexual, psychological or economic harm or suffering, including threats, coercion or arbitrary deprivation of liberty, in public or private life."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It is directed against a woman because she is a woman or affects women disproportionately; it is both a form of discrimination and a human rights violation, rooted in unequal power relations. It covers manifestations such as domestic, sexual, obstetric or vicarious violence, as well as harmful practices such as female genital mutilation. For women and girls with disabilities, it takes specific and aggravated forms – forced sterilisation or treatment, institutional violence or violence by caregivers – and intersects with disability-based discrimination; hence the need for reinforced measures of prevention, protection, accessibility and access to justice (art. 16 CRPD)."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-13> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/digital-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/economic-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/institutional-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/obstetric-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/physical-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/psychological-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/second-order-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/vicarious-violence> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/forced-sterilisation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-based-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/caregiver-violence> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/digital-violence> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "digital violence"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.8.1" ;
  skos:definition "Gender-based acts committed or aggravated via Information and Communication Technologies (ICT)."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/violence-against-women> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-based-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-harassment>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/hate-speech> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/economic-violence> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "economic violence"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.8.2" ;
  skos:definition "The use of control, exploitation, or sabotage to restrict a person’s ability to earn, access, use, or retain financial resources, undermining their economic independence"@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Economic control may include restricting access to money, necessities, financial assets, and information. Economic exploitation may include opening a line of credit in the partner’s name without consent or building up debt in her name, stealing or damaging her property or resources, or selling them without permission, and taking her wages, pensions, or other forms of financial aid without permission. Economic sabotage may include preventing the partner from attending employment or educational activities, sabotaging items needed for such activities, interfering with the partner’s work or education, and refusing to contribute to childcare responsibilities. ICT can facilitate certain forms of economic violence, especially economic control and exploitation."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/violence-against-women> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/independence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/feminisation-of-poverty>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/employment> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/institutional-violence> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "institutional violence"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.8.3" ;
  skos:definition "Harm caused or intensified by an institution’s policies, practices, or failure to act, for example when they deny protection, reinforce discrimination, or further harm people seeking help."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It may include actions, omissions, or lack of due diligence by public authorities that obstruct access to rights, including discredited theories like Parental Alienation Syndrome."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/violence-against-women> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/revictimisation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/access-to-justice> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/obstetric-violence> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "obstetric violence"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.8.4" ;
  skos:definition "Over-medicalisation mistreatment and violence experienced by women in reproductive health services during pregnancy, childbirth, and postpartum."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The term, which emerged in Latin America, covers interventions without consent –caesareans, episiotomies, inductions or unnecessary examinations –, dehumanising or humiliating treatment, lack of information and privacy, or the use of coercive measures. The Special Rapporteur on violence against women described it as a form of gender-based violence and a human rights violation, rooted in stereotypes and power imbalances within health care, and the CEDAW Committee expressly links it to the absence of informed consent and to discrimination through gender stereotypes. It particularly affects women with disabilities, exposed to forced treatments and to barriers of accessibility and understandable information; hence the requirement of free and informed consent and of support in decision-making (arts. 12, 17 and 25 CRPD)."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/violence-against-women> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/free-and-informed-consent>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/forced-sterilisation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reproductive-rights> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/physical-violence> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "physical violence"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.8.5" ;
  skos:definition "Any act or omission that causes physical harm, injury, or bodily suffering to a woman, or places her physical integrity at risk, regardless of whether the injury requires medical treatment."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/violence-against-women> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/abuse>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/domestic-violence> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/psychological-violence> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "psychological violence"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.8.6" ;
  skos:definition "Any intentional conduct, whether active or passive, that seriously harms another person's psychological well-being and self-esteem through the infliction of emotional distress, devaluation, coercion, humiliation, or severe disrespect."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It is characterised by a sustained pattern of abuse that may include repeated insults, verbal aggression, manipulation, threats, intimidation, control over daily activities, forced isolation from social networks, and restrictions on opportunities for personal, social, and economic development."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/violence-against-women> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/abuse>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-isolation>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/trauma> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/second-order-violence> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "second-order violence"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.8.7" ;
  skos:definition "Harassment or discrimination directed against those who support or advocate for victims of gender-based violence."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/violence-against-women> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/harassment>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-based-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support-network> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-violence> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "sexual violence"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.8.8" ;
  skos:definition "Any act violating sexual freedom and dignity by imposing practices without consent, explicitly including penetration, mutilation, forced marriages, and trafficking."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/violence-against-women> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/free-and-informed-consent>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-rights>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/sexual-harassment> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/structural-violence> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "structural violence"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.8.9" ;
  skos:definition "A form of indirect violence that does not necessarily manifest itself through physical aggression or the intentional actions of an individual, but rather is embedded in the social, political, economic, and cultural structures that organise a society."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "These structures generate systematic inequalities that prevent certain individuals or groups from satisfying their basic needs or fully developing their capabilities"@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/violence-against-women> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/institutional-violence> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/vicarious-violence> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "vicarious violence"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.8.10" ;
  skos:definition "A form of gender-based violence that instrumentalises the children – or other persons close to the woman – in order to make her suffer and to control her, and which may reach the point of serious harm or death."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/violence-against-women> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/child-custody>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/best-interests-of-the-child> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/withdrawal-of-support> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "withdrawal of support"@en ;
  skos:notation "13.9" ;
  skos:definition "The intentional withdrawal of assistance with communication, mobility, medication, hygiene or daily living as a form of punishment or control."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-13>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-1> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/support>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/caregiver-violence>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/abuse> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/compulsory-hiring> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "compulsory hiring"@en ;
  skos:notation "14.1" ;
  skos:definition "A set of statutory obligations imposed on employers requiring them to recruit and employ a minimum quota of individuals belonging to designated protected or disadvantaged groups."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "These obligations are typically grounded in labour and anti-discrimination law and are designed to promote substantive equality by addressing structural disadvantages in access to the labour market. Compulsory hiring schemes usually operate through quota-based mechanisms. From a legal perspective, compulsory hiring represents a form of affirmative action justified as an exception to the principle of formal equality in order to achieve effective equality in practice. Within the European legal framework, such measures are considered compatible with the principle of equal treatment. as articulated in Directive 2000/78/EC."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-14> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/hiring-quotas> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/equality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/affirmative-actions> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/hiring-quotas> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "hiring quotas"@en ;
  skos:notation "14.1.1" ;
  skos:definition "legally or institutionally established requirements that mandate employers to ensure that a specified proportion or number of their workforce is composed of individuals from particular groups, typically those who are underrepresented or disadvantaged in the labour market (such as persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, or women)."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "In legal terms, hiring quotas are a form of affirmative action designed to achieve substantive equality (by correcting structural imbalances in employment. They operate by setting measurable targets. Within the European legal framework, hiring quotas are generally considered compatible with the principle of equal treatment where they pursue legitimate social aims and comply with proportionality requirements, as recognised under Directive 2000/78/EC."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/compulsory-hiring> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/affirmative-actions>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/equality> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/decent-work> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "decent work"@en ;
  skos:notation "14.2" ;
  skos:definition "Employment with rights, fair remuneration, safety, social protection, accessibility and freedom from violence and discrimination."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-14> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/employment>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/employment-discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reasonable-accommodation-in-employment--employment-law> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/employment> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "employment"@en ;
  skos:notation "14.3" ;
  skos:definition "A specific form of paid work carried out within an employment relationship, generally governed by labour law, contractual arrangements, and social protection systems."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Employment may be full-time, part-time, temporary, permanent, or flexible and constitutes one of several possible forms of participation in economic life."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-14> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/employability>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/employment-displacement> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/work>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/paid-work>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/decent-work> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/employability> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "employability"@en ;
  skos:notation "14.3.1" ;
  skos:definition "Combination of factors which enable individuals to progress towards or get into employment, to stay in employment and to progress during their careers."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Employability of individuals depends on: i) personal attributes (including adequacy of knowledge and skills); ii) how these personal attributes are presented on the labour market; iii) environmental and social contexts (incentives and opportunities offered to update and validate their knowledge and skills); and iv) the economic context."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/employment> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/lifelong-learning>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusion--employment> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/employment-displacement> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "employment displacement"@en ;
  skos:notation "14.3.2" ;
  skos:definition "The loss or inability to secure or maintain jobs due to barriers like discrimination, lack of accessibility, or limited opportunities, impacting their economic participation and social inclusion."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/employment> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/employment-discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/accessibility>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusion--employment> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/employment-discrimination> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "employment discrimination"@en ;
  skos:notation "14.4" ;
  skos:definition "Unequal treatment in access to employment, recruitment, pay, promotion, training, working conditions or dismissal."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-14> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/glass-ceiling>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/pay-gap> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reasonable-accommodation-in-employment--employment-law> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/glass-ceiling> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "glass ceiling"@en ;
  skos:notation "14.4.1" ;
  skos:definition "Invisible barriers that hinder women’s access to positions of responsibility and power."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/employment-discrimination> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-inequality>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-stereotypes> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/pay-gap> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "pay gap"@en ;
  skos:notation "14.4.2" ;
  skos:definition "A difference in income associated with gender, disability, origin or other inequalities."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/employment-discrimination> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-gap>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/feminisation-of-poverty> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusion--employment> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "inclusion (employment)"@en ;
  skos:notation "14.5" ;
  skos:altLabel "labour inclusion"@en ;
  skos:definition "Labour inclusion refers to equal treatment in the workplace, both when accessing a job and in the subsequent development of a professional career."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "This treatment ensures that people in vulnerable conditions – regardless of their ethnic origin, gender, sexual orientation, or any other circumstance – have the same opportunities to access dignified employment. Labor inclusion is based on the premise that the only requirement an individual should meet to obtain a job is their qualification and ability to perform it, without considering anything else."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-14> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/decent-work>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reasonable-accommodation-in-employment--employment-law>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/inclusion--law> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/productive-labour> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "productive labour"@en ;
  skos:notation "14.6" ;
  skos:definition "Market-oriented activities recognised through wages, income, employment status, or economic accounting."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-14> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reproductive-labour> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/reproductive-labour> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "reproductive labour"@en ;
  skos:notation "14.7" ;
  skos:definition "Paid or unpaid work required to sustain persons, households, communities, and the labour force, such as care, domestic work, child-rearing, and emotional support."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Productive and reproductive labour are distinct but interdependent forms of work.The distinction is neither natural nor neutral. Historically, economic and legal frameworks have treated remunerated market activity as productive while excluding or undervaluing domestic and care work, disproportionately performed by women. This reflects patriarchal divisions of labour that have represented reproductive work as a private duty or a natural female role rather than as socially necessary labour."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-14> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/productive-labour>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/care-work>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/unpaid-work>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-role> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/welfare-benefit> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "welfare benefit"@en ;
  skos:notation "14.8" ;
  skos:definition "Public or social security benefits and social assistance measures granted under national and EU law to protect individuals against social risks – such as unemployment, illness, disability, family responsibilities, or old age – and to promote social inclusion and a decent standard of living."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Under EU law, these benefits form part of the broader concept of social security and social assistance recognised by Article 34 of the Charter of Fundamental Rights of the European Union1, which entitles legally resident persons to social security benefits and social advantages in accordance with Union and national legislation. Access to these benefits must respect the principle of equal treatment and must not be restricted on discriminatory grounds, including nationality and residence status, unless justified under EU law."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-14> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/poverty>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/social-inclusion>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/independent-living-and-inclusion-in-the-community> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/work> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "work"@en ;
  skos:notation "14.9" ;
  skos:definition "The broad range of activities through which persons produce goods and services, provide care, sustain households and communities, develop capacities and identities, and participate in economic and social life."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Work may be paid or unpaid and may take place in formal or informal, public, private, household, community, or voluntary settings. The concept therefore extends beyond employment and market-oriented activity to include forms of domestic, caring, subsistence, creative, and community work that are socially necessary but often insufficiently recognised or valued."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-14> ;
  skos:narrower <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/care-work>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/paid-work>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/unpaid-work> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/employment> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/care-work> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "care work"@en ;
  skos:notation "14.9.1" ;
  skos:definition "paid or unpaid activities involved in meeting the physical, emotional, relational, and practical needs of other persons."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It includes direct forms of care, such as assisting children, older persons, persons with disabilities, or persons who are ill, as well as the domestic and organisational activities that make care possible. Care work is essential to individual well-being and to the everyday reproduction of households, communities, and society. Because care responsibilities are unequally distributed and often assigned to women, they may contribute to inequalities in income, employment, education, leisure, and participation."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/work> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/care>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/caregiver> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/paid-work> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "paid work"@en ;
  skos:notation "14.9.2" ;
  skos:definition "Work performed in exchange for wages, salaries, fees, income, or other forms of monetary remuneration."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It may take place within formal or informal labour markets and includes employment, self-employment, freelance work, entrepreneurship, and other income-generating activities. Paid work may provide access to income, social protection, professional recognition, and labour rights, although these benefits depend on working conditions, remuneration, legal status, and the availability of institutional protections."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/work> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/employment>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/unpaid-work>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/care-work> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .

<https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/unpaid-work> a skos:Concept ;
  skos:prefLabel "unpaid work"@en ;
  skos:notation "14.9.3" ;
  skos:definition "Work that sustains persons, households, communities, and society, and is performed without direct monetary remuneration"@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "It includes domestic work, care work, subsistence activities, family work, volunteering, and community support. Although it produces substantial social and economic value, it is frequently excluded or undervalued in labour statistics, systems of social protection, and conventional measures of economic production. Its unequal distribution, particularly along gendered lines, may restrict access to income, education, leisure, public participation, and paid employment."@en ;
  skos:broader <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/work> ;
  skos:related <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/care-work>, <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/caregiver> ;
  skos:inScheme <https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/> .
